export const GENERATED_GUIDES: Record<string, GeneratedGuide> = {
  "flow-format-spec": {
    "title": "Flow format specification",
    "description": "The JSON format of a SendSeven Flow definition: trigger, nodes, edges, entry node, every node type and its fields. Read before writing or editing a flow for validate_flow / save_flow_draft.",
    "text": "# Flow format spec\n\nThis page describes every part of a flow `definition`: node types, their fields and allowed values, condition operators, variables, edges and triggers. It is generated from the same rules the API uses to validate flows, so a definition that follows it saves cleanly.\n\nUse it to:\n\n- read a flow as JSON with `GET /api/v1/flows/{flow_id}` (the `definition` field),\n- create or update flows with `POST /api/v1/flows` and `PATCH /api/v1/flows/{flow_id}` (same validation as a save in the builder),\n- let an AI write the JSON for you (copy the prompt below).\n\n## Prompt for your AI\n\nCopy this prompt into your AI assistant, then describe the flow you want. It points the AI at the Markdown version of this page, so the AI always works from the current spec.\n\n```text\nYou write SendSeven flow definitions as JSON.\nBefore you write or change a flow, read the full, current SendSeven flow format spec:\nhttps://docs.sendseven.com/guides/flows/flow-format-spec.md\nIt is plain Markdown and lists every node type, field, branch label and trigger.\nFollow the spec exactly:\n- Output ONE JSON object with keys schema_version (1), trigger, entry_node_id, nodes, edges.\n- Every node is {\"id\", \"type\", \"config\"} plus an optional \"display_name\" (unique).\n  \"position\" ({\"x\", \"y\"}) is optional; omit it, the builder lays flows out left to right automatically.\n- Use only the node types, fields and branch labels listed in the spec. Never invent keys.\n- entry_node_id is the first action node (never the trigger).\n- Every edge is {\"from\", \"to\", \"branch\"}. Use the exact branch labels of the source node.\n- A send step's button output is \"btn_<button id>\", never a caption.\n- A menu many steps return to is ONE step that others connect back to; do not copy it.\n- For a long flow you may add \"layout\": {\"version\": 1, \"sections_enabled\": true,\n  \"suggested_sections\": true, \"section_names\": {\"<step id>\": \"Main menu\"}}. Nothing else in layout.\n- Variables are {{namespace.field}}, e.g. {{contact.first_name}} or {{vars.order.total}}.\n- Never invent ids for channels, team members, inboxes, lists, bots, templates or flows.\n  Leave the field out (or ask me) unless I gave you a real id.\n- Tags in add_tags, remove_tags and has_tag rules are tag NAMES, not ids.\n- Put API credentials in {{secrets.NAME}}, never inline.\nReturn only the JSON, no commentary.\n```\n\n## Top-level shape\n\n```json\n{\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"trigger\": {\n    \"type\": \"keyword_match\",\n    \"config\": {\n      \"keywords\": [\n        \"hi\"\n      ],\n      \"match_mode\": \"contains\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"entry_node_id\": \"first_step\",\n  \"nodes\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"first_step\",\n      \"type\": \"send\",\n      \"display_name\": \"Greeting\",\n      \"config\": {}\n    }\n  ],\n  \"edges\": [\n    {\n      \"from\": \"first_step\",\n      \"to\": \"next_step\",\n      \"branch\": \"default\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n| Key | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `schema_version` | Always `1`. |\n| `trigger` | `{type, config}`. See *Triggers*. |\n| `entry_node_id` | The first action node. It must not be a trigger node. A legacy `{type: \"trigger\"}` node is removed on save: `entry_node_id` moves to its target and its config becomes `trigger` when that is missing. |\n| `nodes` | Array of `{id, type, config}`. `id` is any unique string. |\n| `edges` | Array of `{from, to, branch}`. `branch` must be a label of the source node type. |\n| `inbound_behavior` | `ignore` / `stop_and_open_conversation` (what an inbound message does while the run waits). |\n| `allow_stop_keyword`, `stop_keyword_response` | Let the contact stop the flow with a keyword; the response is Send-shaped. |\n| `global_timeout_enabled`, `global_timeout_seconds`, `global_timeout_node_id` | Move an inactive run to a node after 60..2592000 seconds. |\n| `suppress_inbound_conversation` | Boolean. Do not open an inbox conversation for messages that start this flow. |\n| `ignored_warning_codes` | Optional list of warning codes to hide for this flow. Only codes marked ignorable in *Warning codes* stay; others are removed on save. Never changes how the flow runs. |\n| `layout` | Optional. How the builder draws the flow (sections, pinned positions). Never changes what the flow does. See *Layout and sections*. |\n\nPer node, only `id`, `type`, `config` and the optional `display_name` (unique per flow) matter. `position` (`{x, y}`) is optional and only affects where the builder draws the node; a missing position never causes a validation error.\n\nLimits: the definition may be at most 256 KB and nested at most 64 levels deep.\n\nChannels are not set per node. The flow's `default_channel_chain` (a field of the flow, next to `definition`) sets the channel order, using `email` / `instagram` / `live_chat` / `messenger` / `rcs` / `sms` / `telegram` / `whatsapp`.\n\n## Node types\n\nAllowed `type` values: `api_call` / `branch` / `choice` / `close_conversation` / `code` / `collect_input` / `condition` / `create_tracked_link` / `delay` / `end_flow` / `hubspot_set_field` / `kb_query` / `open_conversation` / `run_assistant` / `send` / `send_email` / `send_template` / `send_whatsapp_flow` / `start_flow` / `tag`. Any other type is rejected.\n\n### `api_call`\n\nCalls an external HTTPS API and stores the response.\n\n| Field | Type / allowed values | Required | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `method` | `DELETE` / `GET` / `HEAD` / `PATCH` / `POST` / `PUT` | yes |  |\n| `url` | https URL (may use variables) | yes | Private, loopback and metadata addresses are refused. |\n| `auth` | `{type, ...}` | no | `type`: `api_key` / `basic` / `bearer` / `none`. Put credentials in `{{secrets.NAME}}`, never inline. |\n| `headers` | array of `{key, value}` | no |  |\n| `query_params` | array of `{key, value}` | no |  |\n| `body_mode` | `form` / `json` / `none` / `raw` | no |  |\n| `body_json` | JSON (may use variables) | no |  |\n| `timeout_seconds` | integer 1..30 | no |  |\n| `output_var` | variable name | no | Response at `{{vars.<name>.status}}`, `{{vars.<name>.body...}}`. |\n| `retry_on_error` | boolean | no |  |\n\n**Branches:** `default` / `error` / `success`.\n\n### `branch`\n\nMulti-way switch: the first case whose rules match wins, otherwise `else`.\n\n| Field | Type / allowed values | Required | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `cases` | array (1..20) of `{rules_op, rules[], label?}` | yes | Same rule shape as the Condition node. |\n| `else_node_id` | node id | no | Alternative to an `else` edge. |\n\n**Branches:** `case:0`, `case:1`, ... (0-based, one per case) and `else` (`default` is an alias of `else`).\n\n### `choice`\n\nAsks a question with 2 to 10 buttons and routes on the picked option.\n\n| Field | Type / allowed values | Required | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `prompt` | Send-shaped object (`messages[]`, see the Send node) | yes |  |\n| `options` | array (2..10) of `{id, label}` | yes | Each `id` becomes an edge branch. |\n| `attempt_timeout_seconds` | integer 60..604800 | no | Default 86400. |\n| `on_timeout_node_id` | node id | no |  |\n\n**Branches:** One edge per option `id`, plus `fallback` (unrecognised reply) and `timeout`.\n\n### `close_conversation`\n\nCloses the current conversation and continues.\n\n| Field | Type / allowed values | Required | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `reason` | string | no |  |\n\n**Branches:** `default`.\n\n### `code`\n\nRuns a short sandboxed script over the flow variables.\n\n| Field | Type / allowed values | Required | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `code` | string, max 64 KB | yes | No network or file access. |\n| `timeout_seconds` | integer 1..10 | no |  |\n| `declared_outputs` | array of variable names | no | Values the script returns into `vars`. |\n| `error_var` | variable name | no |  |\n\n**Branches:** `default` / `error` / `success`.\n\n### `collect_input`\n\nAsks a question, waits for the reply, validates it and stores it.\n\n| Field | Type / allowed values | Required | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `prompt` | Send-shaped object (`messages[]`, see the Send node) | yes | The question. Never put a `channel_chain` inside it. |\n| `target_field` | `contact.<column>`, `contact.custom.<key>` or `flow.var.<key>` | no | Built-in columns: `contact.birthday` / `contact.country` / `contact.email` / `contact.first_name` / `contact.language` / `contact.last_name` / `contact.phone`. |\n| `validation` | `{type, ...}` | no | `type`: `date` / `email` / `enum` / `file` / `number` / `phone` / `regex` / `text`. For `file`, `accepted_kinds` is a subset of `any` / `audio` / `document` / `image` / `video`. |\n| `validation_error_message` | Send-shaped object (`messages[]`, see the Send node) | no | Sent when the reply is invalid. |\n| `max_attempts` | integer 1..10 | no |  |\n| `attempt_timeout_seconds` | integer 60..604800 | no | Default 86400 (24 hours). |\n| `on_timeout_node_id` | node id | no |  |\n\n**Branches:** `default` (valid answer) and `timeout`. With buttons: one edge per button id plus `fallback`.\n\n### `condition`\n\nBinary yes/no gate over one or more rules.\n\n| Field | Type / allowed values | Required | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `rules_op` | `all` / `any` | no | Default `all`. |\n| `rules` | array of rule objects (non-empty) | yes | Each rule: `field`, `operator`, `value` (omitted for value-less operators), optional `value_type`. See *Condition rules* below. |\n| `wait_max_seconds` | integer 0..1209600 | no | Re-check for up to this long before taking `no`. |\n\n**Branches:** `yes` and `no` (both required at publish). Legacy aliases accepted: `0` / `1` / `default` / `false` / `true`.\n\n### `create_tracked_link`\n\nCreates a click-tracked short link and stores it in a flow variable.\n\n| Field | Type / allowed values | Required | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `original_url` | URL (may use variables) | yes |  |\n| `output_var_name` | variable name | yes | Read it later as `{{vars.<name>}}`. |\n| `link_label` | string, max 255 | no |  |\n| `expires_in_days` | integer 1..3650 | no |  |\n\n**Branches:** `default`. Use the Condition operator `link_clicked` on `flow.var.<name>` to branch on a click.\n\n### `delay`\n\nPauses the run without blocking (scheduler based), from seconds to weeks.\n\n| Field | Type / allowed values | Required | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `mode` | `time` / `event` | no | Default `time`. |\n| `duration_seconds` | integer, 0 or more | yes for `time` | How long to wait. |\n| `event` | `reply` | no | `event` mode only: wait for the contact's next reply. |\n| `channel_type` | string | no | `event` mode only: only a reply on this channel type resumes. |\n| `timeout_seconds` | integer | no | `event` mode only: give up after this many seconds. |\n\n**Branches:** `default`.\n\n### `end_flow`\n\nEnds the run.\n\n| Field | Type / allowed values | Required | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `reason` | string | no |  |\n| `close_conversation` | boolean | no | Default false. |\n\n**Branches:** None (terminal).\n\n### `hubspot_set_field`\n\nWrites a property on the linked HubSpot contact.\n\n| Field | Type / allowed values | Required | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `property_name` | HubSpot property | yes |  |\n| `value` | string (may use variables) | yes |  |\n| `output_var` | variable name | no |  |\n\n**Branches:** `default` / `error` / `no_mapping` / `success`.\n\n### `kb_query`\n\nSearches the Knowledge Base and stores the answer.\n\n| Field | Type / allowed values | Required | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `query` | string (may use variables) | yes |  |\n| `folder_ids` | array of folder ids | no |  |\n| `top_k` | integer 1..10 | no |  |\n| `output_var` | variable name | yes | Read it later as `{{vars.<name>}}`. |\n| `min_confidence` | number | no |  |\n| `ask_first` | boolean | no | Ask the contact for the question first (uses `prompt`). |\n| `timeout_seconds` | integer | no |  |\n\n**Branches:** `default` / `match` / `no_match` / `timeout`.\n\n### `open_conversation`\n\nOpens (or reuses) a conversation in the inbox and optionally assigns it.\n\n| Field | Type / allowed values | Required | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `assign_strategy` | `contact_owner` / `least_busy_by_tag` / `least_busy_in_team` / `none` / `round_robin_all` / `round_robin_by_tag` / `round_robin_in_set` / `round_robin_in_team` / `specific_user` | no | Default `none`. |\n| `assign_user_id` | team member id | for `specific_user` | Must be a member of this workspace. |\n| `assign_team_id` | inbox id | for team strategies | Must be an inbox of this workspace. |\n| `assignment_tag_id` | member tag id | for skill routing |  |\n| `prefer_online_agents` | boolean | no |  |\n| `add_conversation_tags` | array of tag names | no |  |\n| `remove_conversation_tags` | array of tag names | no |  |\n| `unattended_fallback_minutes` | integer 1..1440 | no | Tag-based strategies only: re-route when nobody has picked the conversation up in time. |\n\n**Branches:** `default`.\n\n### `run_assistant`\n\nHands the conversation to an AI bot or Conversational Agent until it finishes.\n\n| Field | Type / allowed values | Required | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `bot_id` | bot id | yes for a bot | Must be a bot of this workspace. |\n| `target` | `{type, id}` | no | `type`: `agent` / `bot`. |\n| `timeout_seconds` | integer | no |  |\n| `max_turns` | integer 1..200 | no |  |\n| `allow_stop_keyword` | boolean | no |  |\n| `output_variable_prefix` | identifier | no | Prefix for the values the assistant extracts. |\n\n**Branches:** `completed` / `default` / `done` / `escalated` / `timeout`. `done` is only valid when the target is an agent.\n\n### `send`\n\nSends one or more messages to the contact over the flow's channel chain.\n\n| Field | Type / allowed values | Required | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `messages` | array of message objects | yes (unless `channel_content` covers every channel) | Each message: `type` (`audio` / `document` / `image` / `interactive` / `text` / `video`), `text`, `order` (integer, required), `delay_seconds` (0..15, required), optional `media_url` or `attachment_id` (required for media types at publish), `media_caption`, `buttons[]` (`id`, `label`). |\n| `channel_content` | object keyed by channel type | no | Per-channel override: `{\"whatsapp\": {\"messages\": [...]}}`. Channels without an entry use `messages`. |\n| `button_wait_seconds` | integer 60..604800 | no | Only when a message has buttons: how long to wait for a click. |\n| `timeout_enabled` | boolean | no | Default true. False waits for a click without a deadline. |\n| `on_timeout_node_id` | node id | no | Where to continue when the button wait times out. |\n\n**Branches:** Without buttons: `default` (next step, required). With buttons: one edge `btn_<id>` per button `id` (see *Output keys*), `default` (\"Other reply\": the contact typed instead of tapping; optional), `timeout` (required while `timeout_enabled` is true), and the optional `fallback` and `error` (\"Not delivered\"). URL buttons have no output. Template buttons use `tplbtn_<index>`.\n\n### `send_email`\n\nSends an email from one of the workspace's mailboxes.\n\n| Field | Type / allowed values | Required | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `to` | email address or variable | no | Default is the contact's email (`to_source`). |\n| `to_source` | string | no |  |\n| `subject` | string (may use variables) | yes |  |\n| `email_mailbox_id` | mailbox id | yes at publish |  |\n| `body_mode` | string | no | Plain text, HTML or a saved email design. |\n| `body_html` | string | no |  |\n| `body_text` | string | no |  |\n| `email_content_id` | saved email design id | no |  |\n\n**Branches:** `default`.\n\n### `send_template`\n\nSends an approved WhatsApp template (works outside the 24-hour window).\n\n| Field | Type / allowed values | Required | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `template_id` | WhatsApp template id | yes | Must be a template of this workspace. |\n| `template_variables` | object | no | Values for the template placeholders (may use variables). |\n| `buttons` | array of `{index, caption}` | no | Snapshot of the template's quick-reply buttons. |\n| `attempt_timeout_seconds` | integer 60..604800 | no |  |\n| `on_timeout_node_id` | node id | no |  |\n\n**Branches:** `tplbtn_<index>` per button, `fallback`, `timeout`, `default`.\n\n### `send_whatsapp_flow`\n\nSends a WhatsApp Flow (form) and waits for the submission.\n\n| Field | Type / allowed values | Required | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `whatsapp_flow_id` | WhatsApp Flow id | yes | Must be a WhatsApp Flow of this workspace. |\n| `output_key` | variable name | no | Submitted answers land under this key. |\n| `attempt_timeout_seconds` | integer 60..604800 | no |  |\n\n**Branches:** `completed` / `default` / `fallback` / `timeout`.\n\n### `start_flow`\n\nStarts another flow of this workspace, optionally waiting for its result.\n\n| Field | Type / allowed values | Required | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `target_flow_id` | flow id | yes | Must be a flow of this workspace. |\n| `mode` | `wait` / `fire_and_forget` | no |  |\n| `input_vars` | array of `{name, value}` | no |  |\n| `output_mapping` | array of `{from, to}` | no | `wait` mode: copy the child's variables back. |\n| `result_variable` | variable name | no |  |\n\n**Branches:** `wait`: `completed` / `default` / `failed` / `timeout`. `fire_and_forget`: `default` / `failed`.\n\n### `tag`\n\nUpdates the contact: tags, fields, subscriptions and languages.\n\n| Field | Type / allowed values | Required | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `add_tags` | array of tag names | no | Tags are matched by NAME, case-insensitively. |\n| `remove_tags` | array of tag names | no |  |\n| `set_fields` | array of `{field, value}` | no | `field` is a built-in (`birthday` / `company` / `email` / `first_name` / `language` / `last_name` / `lifecycle_stage` / `phone` / `source`) or `custom.<key>`. `value` may use variables. |\n| `subscriptions` | array of `{list_id, channel_type, action}` | no | Subscribe or unsubscribe the contact. |\n| `languages_add` | array of language codes | no |  |\n| `languages_remove` | array of language codes | no |  |\n\n**Branches:** `default`.\n\n## Condition rules\n\nUsed by `condition` and by every case of `branch`.\n\n```json\n{\"field\": \"contact.tag\", \"operator\": \"has_tag\", \"value\": \"VIP\"}\n{\"field\": \"vars.api_result.body.count\", \"operator\": \"gt\", \"value\": \"10\", \"value_type\": \"number\"}\n```\n\n**`field`** is one of:\n\n- a curated field: `contact.has_method` / `contact.tag` / `flow.entry_at` / `flow.last_button_clicked` / `flow.last_channel_used` / `flow.last_inbound` / `subscription`,\n- a dotted field under `contact.` / `flow.var.` (for example `contact.first_name`, `contact.custom.plan`, `flow.var.answer`),\n- a variable path whose first part is `contact` / `conversation` / `flow` / `message` / `trigger` / `vars` (for example `vars.api_result.body.count`). `secrets` is never allowed in a condition.\n\nAn unknown namespace is rejected with a path such as `nodes[route].cases[0].rules[0].field`.\n\n**`operator`** is one of `after` / `before` / `contains` / `contains_any` / `ends_with` / `eq` / `equals` / `exists` / `gt` / `gte` / `has_contact_method_for_channel` / `has_tag` / `is_empty` / `is_false` / `is_not_empty` / `is_subscribed_to_list` / `is_true` / `language_identified` / `language_in` / `language_is` / `language_not` / `language_not_in` / `language_unidentified` / `link_clicked` / `lt` / `lte` / `more_than_days_ago` / `neq` / `not_contains` / `not_equals` / `not_exists` / `not_has_contact_method_for_channel` / `not_has_tag` / `not_one_of` / `not_subscribed_to_list` / `on` / `one_of` / `starts_with` / `within_last_days`.\n\nOperators without a `value`: `exists` / `has_contact_method_for_channel` / `is_empty` / `is_false` / `is_not_empty` / `is_subscribed_to_list` / `is_true` / `language_identified` / `language_unidentified` / `link_clicked` / `not_exists` / `not_has_contact_method_for_channel` / `not_subscribed_to_list`.\n\n| Operator | Only valid on `field` |\n|---|---|\n| `link_clicked` | `flow.var.<name>` |\n| `has_contact_method_for_channel` | `contact.has_method` |\n| `is_subscribed_to_list` | `subscription` |\n| `language_identified` | `contact.language` |\n| `language_in` | `contact.language` |\n| `language_is` | `contact.language` |\n| `language_not` | `contact.language` |\n| `language_not_in` | `contact.language` |\n| `language_unidentified` | `contact.language` |\n| `not_has_contact_method_for_channel` | `contact.has_method` |\n| `not_subscribed_to_list` | `subscription` |\n\nTag rules (`has_tag`, `not_has_tag`) compare the tag NAME, case-insensitively.\n`one_of`, `not_one_of` and `contains_any` take a JSON array of strings as `value`.\nLanguage operators (`language_identified` / `language_in` / `language_is` / `language_not` / `language_not_in` / `language_unidentified`) take ISO codes and accept `include_unidentified: true`.\n\n**`value_type`** (optional) is one of `auto` / `boolean` / `date` / `number` / `string`. With an explicit type, the operator must fit it and a literal `value` must parse as that type:\n\n| `value_type` | Operators |\n|---|---|\n| `auto` | `after` / `before` / `contains` / `contains_any` / `ends_with` / `eq` / `exists` / `gt` / `gte` / `is_empty` / `is_false` / `is_not_empty` / `is_true` / `lt` / `lte` / `more_than_days_ago` / `neq` / `not_contains` / `not_exists` / `not_one_of` / `on` / `one_of` / `starts_with` / `within_last_days` |\n| `string` | `contains` / `contains_any` / `ends_with` / `eq` / `exists` / `gt` / `gte` / `is_empty` / `is_not_empty` / `lt` / `lte` / `neq` / `not_contains` / `not_exists` / `not_one_of` / `one_of` / `starts_with` |\n| `number` | `eq` / `exists` / `gt` / `gte` / `is_empty` / `is_not_empty` / `lt` / `lte` / `neq` / `not_exists` |\n| `boolean` | `eq` / `exists` / `is_false` / `is_true` / `neq` / `not_exists` |\n| `date` | `after` / `before` / `eq` / `exists` / `gt` / `gte` / `is_empty` / `is_not_empty` / `lt` / `lte` / `more_than_days_ago` / `neq` / `not_exists` / `on` / `within_last_days` |\n\n## Variables\n\nText fields may contain variables written as `{{namespace.field}}`. Paths use dots, `[0]` for list items and `[\"key\"]` for keys with special characters.\n\n| Namespace | Contents |\n|---|---|\n| `contact` | The contact: `first_name`, `last_name`, `email`, `phone`, `custom.<key>`, `tags`, owner fields. |\n| `message` | The message that started the run (`text`, `type`). |\n| `conversation` | The current conversation, including `assigned_agent`. |\n| `trigger` | The trigger payload (for example `{{trigger.order_id}}` for an API event). |\n| `vars` | Flow variables written by steps (`output_var`, `target_field: flow.var.<key>`, ...). `flow.var.<key>` is an alias. |\n| `flow` | Run facts: `last_inbound`, `last_button_clicked`, `last_channel_used`, `entry_at`. |\n| `secrets` | Workspace secrets. Only in the `api_call` node; never in messages or conditions. |\n\nUnknown variables render as an empty string. Variable names you create cannot be `is_test` / `stop_flow_url` / `test_conversation_id` / `waflow` and cannot start with `__`.\n\n## Edges and branches\n\nAn edge is `{from, to, branch}`. `from` and `to` are node ids. `branch` selects which exit of the source node the edge belongs to; each node type lists its labels above. Most action nodes use `default`. At publish every reachable node must be connected and every `condition` needs both `yes` and `no`.\n\n## Output keys\n\nThe `branch` of an edge is the output of the source step it leaves from. The rules the builder, the validator and the engine share:\n\n| Output | On | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `btn_<id>` | `send` with reply buttons or list rows | The contact tapped the button whose `id` is `<id>`. One per button; buttons with the same `id` in several messages or channel versions share one output. URL buttons have no output. |\n| `default` | `send` with buttons | \"Other reply\": the contact typed a message instead of tapping. Optional; without it the run ends there. |\n| `default` | steps without choices (`send` without buttons, `tag`, `delay`, `open_conversation`, ...) | \"Next\". Required at publish. |\n| `fallback` | `send` with buttons, `choice`, `collect_input` with buttons | Optional catch-all for a reply that matches nothing. |\n| `timeout` | `send` with buttons and `timeout_enabled`, `choice`, `collect_input`, `run_assistant` | Nobody answered in time. Wire it: when it fires with nothing connected, the run fails. |\n| `error` | `send` that waits for a tap | \"Not delivered\": the message could not be delivered. Optional. |\n| `completed` / `default` / `escalated` / `timeout` | `run_assistant` | Required (`timeout` only while `timeout_enabled` is not false). |\n| `done` | `run_assistant` with an agent target | Optional; falls back to `completed`. |\n\n`choice` and `collect_input` use the bare option or button `id` as the output (no `btn_` prefix). `end` has no outputs.\n\nOlder flows may still use caption keys on send buttons, `btn_` plus the lowercased button text (for example `btn_main menu`). They keep routing while the `btn_<id>` key of that button is not wired; the `btn_<id>` key always wins. The builder rewrites caption keys to `btn_<id>` when it saves, so write `btn_<id>` in new definitions.\n\n## Layout and sections\n\nFlows are drawn left to right: Start on the left, each step's outputs on its right edge, and the steps an output leads to further right. The builder computes the layout from the graph, so you never need `position`. A long flow can be grouped into sections: a section starts at one step (a menu, for example) and holds the steps that follow it. Sections are derived from the graph; the optional `layout` block only stores what the person changed in the builder:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"layout\": {\n    \"version\": 1,\n    \"sections_enabled\": true,\n    \"suggested_sections\": true,\n    \"sections_added\": [\n      \"support_menu\"\n    ],\n    \"sections_removed\": [],\n    \"section_names\": {\n      \"main_menu\": \"Main menu\",\n      \"support_menu\": \"Support\"\n    },\n    \"step_sections\": {\n      \"handover\": \"support_menu\"\n    },\n    \"section_positions\": {\n      \"support_menu\": {\n        \"x\": 1200,\n        \"y\": 80\n      }\n    },\n    \"pinned_steps\": {\n      \"handover\": {\n        \"x\": 330,\n        \"y\": 96\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n| Key | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `version` | `1`: the flow uses the left-to-right layout. |\n| `sections_enabled` | `true` / `false` shows or hides sections. Missing or `null`: the builder decides (long flows with several menus get sections). |\n| `suggested_sections` | Use the sections the builder suggests from the graph (`true` / `false` / `null`). |\n| `sections_added`, `sections_removed` | Step ids where a section was added or removed by hand. |\n| `section_names` | Section name by the id of the step it starts at, at most 80 characters. |\n| `step_sections` | Step id to the id of the section start step it was moved into. |\n| `section_positions`, `pinned_steps` | Hand-placed positions `{x, y}` of a section frame or a step (finite numbers within +/-10,000,000). |\n\nRules: only these keys are allowed (any other key is a 422 with a `layout.<key>` path), each list or map holds at most 2000 entries and a step id is at most 128 characters. `trigger` is the id of the Start step. When you save, entries for step ids that no longer exist are dropped silently. `layout` never affects how a flow runs, and `null` or a missing block is always valid.\n\n## Editing a flow as JSON\n\nThe builder's JSON view shows the flow as one document: the editable fields of the flow (`name`, `description`, `default_channel_chain`, `priority`, `re_entry_policy`, ...) plus `definition`. Apply it, or edit flows from your own code, with `PATCH /api/v1/flows/{flow_id}` and the flow's current `version`:\n\n- read-only fields (`id`, `status`, `validation`, `run_count`, timestamps, ...) are ignored, never an error,\n- a stale `version` returns 409 `flow_version_conflict`; load the flow again and reapply your change,\n- every save adds an entry to the flow's version history (`GET /api/v1/flows/{flow_id}/revisions`).\n\n## Triggers\n\n`trigger` is `{type, config}`. Allowed types:\n\n| Type | Config |\n|---|---|\n| `api_event` | Started by your API call with a payload, read as `{{trigger.<key>}}`. |\n| `click_to_ad` | First message of a conversation that started from an ad. `channels` subset of `instagram` / `messenger` / `whatsapp`, `scope` `any_ad` / `specific_ads`. |\n| `comment_received` | A comment on an Instagram or Facebook post. `match_mode` `contains` / `exact` / `starts_with`, `scope` `all_posts` / `specific_posts`. |\n| `contact.created` | A contact was created. Optional field filters. |\n| `contact.subscribed` | A contact subscribed to a list. |\n| `contact.unsubscribed` | A contact unsubscribed from a list. |\n| `contact.updated` | A contact's fields, tags or subscriptions changed. Optional filters. |\n| `contact_tag_added` | A tag was added to a contact. `tag` (name) or `tag_id`. |\n| `contact_tag_removed` | A tag was removed from a contact. `tag` (name) or `tag_id`. |\n| `conversation_assigned` | A conversation's assignee changed. `assignee_scope`, `user_ids`, `inbox_ids`, `assignment_sources`. |\n| `conversation_closed` | A conversation was closed. Optional `channel_ids`, `tag_filter`. |\n| `conversation_opened` | A conversation was opened. Optional `channel_ids`, `tag_filter`. |\n| `incoming_message` | Every inbound message. Optional `channel_ids`, `tag_filter` `{any_of: [...]}`, `regex`. |\n| `keyword_match` | Inbound message matching `keywords[]` with `match_mode` `contains` / `exact` / `regex`, optional `channel_ids`. |\n| `manual` | Started from the app or `POST /flows/{flow_id}/runs`. No config. |\n| `schedule` | Time based. `schedule_kind`: `birthday` / `recurring_cron` (with `time_of_day_local` / `recurrence_rule`). |\n| `whatsapp_flow.completed` | A contact submitted a WhatsApp Flow form. |\n\nAny event trigger may also carry a business-hours gate `config.schedule` with `mode` `inside` / `outside` and weekly `windows` keyed by `fri` / `mon` / `sat` / `sun` / `thu` / `tue` / `wed`.\n\n## IDs from your workspace\n\nChannel, team member, inbox, list, tag, bot, template and flow ids must belong to the workspace that owns the flow. An id from another workspace (or one that was deleted) is reported as a validation problem on save and blocks publishing. Tags in `add_tags`, `remove_tags` and tag rules are names, so they work across workspaces.\n\n## Validation errors\n\nWhen a definition is invalid, `POST /flows`, `PATCH /flows/{flow_id}` and `POST /flows/{flow_id}/publish` return HTTP 422:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"detail\": {\n    \"code\": \"flow_definition_invalid\",\n    \"message\": \"The flow has 1 problem: nodes[route].cases[0].rules[0].field 'foo.bar' uses unknown namespace 'foo'. ...\",\n    \"errors\": [\n      {\n        \"path\": \"nodes[route].cases[0].rules[0].field\",\n        \"message\": \"nodes[route].cases[0].rules[0].field 'foo.bar' uses unknown namespace 'foo'. ...\"\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`errors[].path` names the node by its `id`. Graph-level problems use an empty path or an `edges[N]`, `trigger` or `entry_node_id` path. Some errors also carry a stable `code` (`entry_node_is_trigger`: the stored flow still uses a trigger node as its entry; saving it again fixes it).\n\nA draft saves as long as its structure is valid. A `published` or `paused` flow is live: a PATCH that changes its `definition` or `default_channel_chain` must pass every publish check, otherwise it is refused with `reason: \"live_flow_invalid\"` and nothing is saved. To keep such an edit, save it as a draft copy with `POST /api/v1/flows/{flow_id}/duplicate` and the body `{\"definition\": ...}`.\n\nKeep node ids stable when you edit a live flow: contacts waiting on a step whose `id` is no longer in the saved definition leave the flow (their run is canceled with `flow_redefined`). Preview how many with `POST /api/v1/flows/{flow_id}/edit-impact` and the body `{\"definition\": ...}`.\n\n## Warning codes\n\nWarnings never block saving, and only `unknown_secret` blocks publishing. They come back in `validation.warnings` as `{path, message, code}`. List an ignorable code in `definition.ignored_warning_codes` to hide that kind of warning for the flow: it is left out of `validation.warnings` and counted in `validation.ignored_warnings_count`. Errors and codes that are not ignorable always show.\n\n| Code | Ignorable | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `whatsapp_window_template_fallback` | yes | WhatsApp step may send outside the 24 h window without a template |\n| `run_assistant_branch_unwired` | yes | AI agent step has an unconnected branch |\n| `duplicate_output_names` | no | AI agent step has duplicate output names |\n| `collect_input_branch_unwired` | yes | Question step has an unconnected button or fallback branch |\n| `collect_input_file_channel` | yes | File question cannot be answered on some channels |\n| `duplicate_button_id` | no | Two buttons or options share the same ID |\n| `collect_input_enum_stale` | yes | \"One of\" values do not match the button captions |\n| `collect_input_enum_redundant` | yes | \"One of\" check is unnecessary because of the buttons |\n| `not_delivered_branch_unused` | yes | \"Not delivered\" branch that is never used |\n| `reserved_error_option_id` | no | Option uses the reserved ID \"error\" |\n| `beta_step_error_branch_unwired` | yes | API call or code step has no error branch |\n| `kb_query_no_match_unwired` | yes | Knowledge base step has no \"no match\" branch |\n| `hubspot_branch_unwired` | yes | HubSpot step has an unconnected error or no-mapping branch |\n| `start_flow_branch_unwired` | yes | Start flow step has an unconnected failed or timeout branch |\n| `whatsapp_flow_branch_unwired` | yes | WhatsApp Flow step has no fallback branch |\n| `whatsapp_flow_no_template` | yes | WhatsApp Flow step has no template for outside the 24 h window |\n| `timeout_branch_unwired` | yes | Waiting step has a timeout but no timeout path |\n| `global_timeout_target_waits` | yes | Flow timeout target is itself a waiting step |\n| `suppress_inbound_catch_all` | yes | Flow hides every incoming message from the inbox |\n| `send_template_no_buttons` | yes | Template step has no quick reply buttons, so every reply goes to fallback |\n| `send_template_missing_params` | yes | Template step does not fill every template variable |\n| `agent_variable_no_fallback` | yes | Assigned agent variable without a fallback value |\n| `literal_credential` | no | Credential typed directly into an API call step |\n| `unknown_secret` | no | Secret is not stored in this workspace (blocks publishing) |\n| `start_flow_target` | yes | Started flow uses other channels or starts another flow |\n| `whatsapp_flow_target` | yes | WhatsApp Flow is archived or a trigger filter points to a deleted form |\n| `assistant_inactive` | yes | Bot or AI agent is inactive |\n| `agent_skill_no_goal` | yes | AI agent skill has no goal |\n| `unsubscribe_list_unknown` | yes | Unsubscribe list no longer exists |\n| `flow_reference` | no | Step points to an item that does not exist in this workspace |\n| `branch_unwired` | yes | Step has an output with no next step |\n| `long_delay` | yes | Delay longer than 90 days |\n| `rule_option_unknown` | yes | Rule value is not an option of the field |\n| `tag_no_actions` | yes | Tag step has no actions |\n| `start_flow_fire_and_forget_waits` | yes | Start flow step competes for replies |\n| `no_exit_path` | yes | Flow has no way for contacts to leave |\n| `stray_branch_edge` | no | Line from an output that no longer exists |\n| `collect_input_max_attempts` | no | Max attempts out of range |\n| `other` | no | Other warning |\n\n`whatsapp_window_template_fallback` fires on a WhatsApp send step without an approved template fallback only when the step can run 24 hours or more after the contact's last message. WhatsApp only allows free-form messages inside that window, and every message from the contact opens it again. A step is treated as safe when every path to it starts with an `incoming_message`, `keyword_match`, `click_to_ad` or `whatsapp_flow.completed` trigger, or passes a step that resumes on the contact's reply (a button tap or list row, a typed reply on the `fallback` or `default` branch, a `choice` or `collect_input` answer, a submitted WhatsApp Flow), and the delays and `timeout` waits after that add up to less than 24 hours. The warning stays when the flow starts from any other trigger (manual, API, schedule, tag, contact or conversation events, comments) before the first reply, on the flow's global timeout step, after a `collect_input` timeout or a `start_flow` that waits, and whenever the timing cannot be worked out.\n\n## Complete example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"trigger\": {\n    \"type\": \"keyword_match\",\n    \"config\": {\n      \"keywords\": [\n        \"pricing\"\n      ],\n      \"match_mode\": \"contains\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"entry_node_id\": \"welcome\",\n  \"nodes\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"welcome\",\n      \"type\": \"send\",\n      \"display_name\": \"Welcome\",\n      \"config\": {\n        \"messages\": [\n          {\n            \"type\": \"text\",\n            \"order\": 0,\n            \"delay_seconds\": 0,\n            \"text\": \"Hi {{contact.first_name}}, happy to help with pricing.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ask_size\",\n      \"type\": \"choice\",\n      \"display_name\": \"Ask team size\",\n      \"config\": {\n        \"prompt\": {\n          \"messages\": [\n            {\n              \"type\": \"text\",\n              \"order\": 0,\n              \"delay_seconds\": 0,\n              \"text\": \"How big is your team?\"\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        \"options\": [\n          {\n            \"id\": \"small\",\n            \"label\": \"1 to 10\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"id\": \"large\",\n            \"label\": \"More than 10\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"attempt_timeout_seconds\": 86400\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"route\",\n      \"type\": \"branch\",\n      \"display_name\": \"Route by tag\",\n      \"config\": {\n        \"cases\": [\n          {\n            \"label\": \"VIP\",\n            \"rules_op\": \"all\",\n            \"rules\": [\n              {\n                \"field\": \"contact.tag\",\n                \"operator\": \"has_tag\",\n                \"value\": \"VIP\"\n              }\n            ]\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"has_email\",\n      \"type\": \"condition\",\n      \"display_name\": \"Has email?\",\n      \"config\": {\n        \"rules\": [\n          {\n            \"field\": \"contact.email\",\n            \"operator\": \"is_not_empty\"\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"tag_lead\",\n      \"type\": \"tag\",\n      \"display_name\": \"Tag as lead\",\n      \"config\": {\n        \"add_tags\": [\n          \"Lead\"\n        ]\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"handover\",\n      \"type\": \"open_conversation\",\n      \"display_name\": \"Hand over to sales\",\n      \"config\": {\n        \"assign_strategy\": \"none\",\n        \"add_conversation_tags\": [\n          \"Sales\"\n        ]\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"small_reply\",\n      \"type\": \"send\",\n      \"display_name\": \"Self-serve answer\",\n      \"config\": {\n        \"messages\": [\n          {\n            \"type\": \"text\",\n            \"order\": 0,\n            \"delay_seconds\": 0,\n            \"text\": \"Our Basic plan fits most small teams.\"\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"done\",\n      \"type\": \"end_flow\",\n      \"display_name\": \"Done\",\n      \"config\": {}\n    }\n  ],\n  \"edges\": [\n    {\n      \"from\": \"welcome\",\n      \"to\": \"ask_size\",\n      \"branch\": \"default\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"from\": \"ask_size\",\n      \"to\": \"small_reply\",\n      \"branch\": \"small\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"from\": \"ask_size\",\n      \"to\": \"route\",\n      \"branch\": \"large\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"from\": \"ask_size\",\n      \"to\": \"done\",\n      \"branch\": \"fallback\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"from\": \"ask_size\",\n      \"to\": \"done\",\n      \"branch\": \"timeout\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"from\": \"route\",\n      \"to\": \"handover\",\n      \"branch\": \"case:0\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"from\": \"route\",\n      \"to\": \"has_email\",\n      \"branch\": \"else\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"from\": \"has_email\",\n      \"to\": \"tag_lead\",\n      \"branch\": \"yes\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"from\": \"has_email\",\n      \"to\": \"handover\",\n      \"branch\": \"no\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"from\": \"tag_lead\",\n      \"to\": \"handover\",\n      \"branch\": \"default\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"from\": \"small_reply\",\n      \"to\": \"done\",\n      \"branch\": \"default\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"from\": \"handover\",\n      \"to\": \"done\",\n      \"branch\": \"default\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```"
  },
  "skills-and-goals": {
    "title": "Writing skills and goals",
    "description": "How to write a skill (slug, description, mode, body, tool allowlist, attachment instruction), versions, and how to add a structured goal (outputs bound to contact fields, validation, completion actions).",
    "text": "# Skills API\n\n:::caution Beta\nConversational Agents are in Beta and available on Professional, Scale and Enterprise.\n\nA **skill** is a reusable instruction set with a stable `slug`. Its content lives in **immutable versions**: every content change creates a new version and makes it current. Agents follow the current version unless their link pins one (see [Skill links](./agents-api.md#skill-links)).\n\nBase URL: `https://api.sendseven.com/api/v1/automation`\n\n| Method | Path | Scope | Success |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `GET` | `/skills` | `automation:read` | `200` paginated list |\n| `POST` | `/skills` | `automation:create` | `201` skill |\n| `POST` | `/skills/import` | `automation:create` **and** `automation:update` | `200` import report |\n| `GET` | `/skills/{skill_id}` | `automation:read` | `200` skill |\n| `PATCH` | `/skills/{skill_id}` | `automation:update` | `200` skill |\n| `DELETE` | `/skills/{skill_id}` | `automation:delete` | `200` `{success, id, action, message}` |\n| `GET` | `/skills/{skill_id}/versions` | `automation:read` | `200` paginated list |\n| `GET` | `/skills/{skill_id}/versions/{version_id}` | `automation:read` | `200` version with body |\n| `POST` | `/skills/{skill_id}/versions/{version_id}/restore` | `automation:update` | `200` skill |\n| `GET` | `/skills/{skill_id}/usage` | `automation:read` | `200` agents and flows using it |\n| `GET` | `/skills/{skill_id}/goal-stats` | `automation:read` | `200` goal statistics |\n\n## Create a skill\n\n```json\n{\n  \"slug\": \"qualify-lead\",\n  \"title\": \"Qualify a lead\",\n  \"description\": \"Use when a visitor asks about pricing, a demo or buying for a team.\",\n  \"default_mode\": \"model_selected\",\n  \"body\": \"Find out the company size, the use case and the timeline. Be brief.\",\n  \"tool_allowlist\": [\"search_knowledge\", \"set_state\"],\n  \"goal\": null,\n  \"attachment_instruction\": {\"preset\": \"read_text\", \"custom_text\": null}\n}\n```\n\n| Field | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `slug` | Optional. 1-64 characters: lowercase letters, digits, `-` and `_`, starting with a letter or digit. Derived from the title when omitted (a number is appended if taken). An explicit slug that is taken returns `409 {\"code\": \"skill_slug_exists\"}`. The slug cannot be changed later. |\n| `title` | **Required.** 1-255 characters. |\n| `description` | **Required.** 1-1,000 characters. Says *when* the skill applies; the agent uses it to choose skills. |\n| `default_mode` | `always_on`, `model_selected` (default) or `flow_only`. See [Skills](./overview.md#skills). |\n| `body` | **Required.** The instructions. Not blank, max 20,000 characters. |\n| `tool_allowlist` | `null` (default) = all of the agent's tools. Otherwise a list of `search_knowledge`, `web_search`, `handoff`, `set_state`, `load_skill`, `complete_task`. See [Skill tool allowlists](./turn-lifecycle.md#skill-tool-allowlists). |\n| `goal` | Optional [skill goal](./skill-goals.md). An invalid goal returns `422 {\"code\": \"invalid_goal\", \"errors\": […]}`. |\n| `attachment_instruction` | Optional (Beta). What the agent does with an image or PDF the contact sends while this skill is loaded. See [Attachment instruction](#attachment-instruction). |\n\n### Attachment instruction\n`{\"preset\": \"…\", \"custom_text\": null}`:\n\n| `preset` | The agent… |\n|---|---|\n| `read_text` | Reads the text in the file carefully (receipts, invoices, error messages, serial or order numbers) and uses the exact values. |\n| `describe` | Describes what is shown (for example product damage or a device setup) and uses it to help. |\n| `context` | Uses the file as extra context for the request. This is the default, also when the field is `null`. |\n| `custom` | Follows your own instruction in `custom_text` (required, 1-1,000 characters). |\n\n`custom_text` is only allowed with `custom`. Invalid values return `422`. The instruction only matters when the agent may read the file type (`read_images` / `read_documents` in [`tools_config`](./agents-api.md#tools-config)); it is also shown to the skill classifier, so skills for receipts or damage photos are picked when a file arrives.\n\nThe response:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"…\",\n  \"slug\": \"qualify-lead\",\n  \"title\": \"Qualify a lead\",\n  \"description\": \"Use when a visitor asks about pricing, a demo or buying for a team.\",\n  \"default_mode\": \"model_selected\",\n  \"current_version\": 1,\n  \"current_version_id\": \"…\",\n  \"is_archived\": false,\n  \"used_by_count\": 0,\n  \"body\": \"Find out the company size, the use case and the timeline. Be brief.\",\n  \"tool_allowlist\": [\"search_knowledge\", \"set_state\"],\n  \"goal\": null,\n  \"attachment_instruction\": {\"preset\": \"read_text\", \"custom_text\": null},\n  \"created_at\": \"2026-10-05T09:00:00Z\",\n  \"updated_at\": \"2026-10-05T09:00:00Z\"\n}\n```\n\nThe list endpoint returns the same fields without `body`, `tool_allowlist`, `goal` and `attachment_instruction`.\n\n## List skills\n\n`GET /skills?search=lead&include_archived=false&page=1&page_size=50`\n\n| Parameter | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `search` | Optional, max 100 characters |\n| `include_archived` | Default `false` |\n| `page`, `page_size` | `page_size` default 50, max 100 |\n\nList items additionally carry:\n\n| Field | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `used_by` | Agents this skill is linked to: `[{\"agent_id\", \"bot_id\", \"name\", \"avatar_url\"}]`. `avatar_url` is a short-lived display URL. |\n| `has_goal` | `true` when the current version has a [goal](./skill-goals.md). |\n| `goal_label` | The goal's objective (max 120 characters), or `null`. |\n\n## Update a skill\n\n`PATCH /skills/{skill_id}` — all fields optional:\n\n| Field | Creates a new version? |\n|---|---|\n| `title`, `description`, `body` | Yes, when the value changes |\n| `tool_allowlist` | Yes. Omit it to keep it; send `null` to allow all tools. |\n| `goal` | Yes. Omit it to keep the current goal; send `null` to remove it. |\n| `attachment_instruction` | Yes. Omit it to keep the current value; send `null` to go back to the default (`context`). |\n| `default_mode` | No |\n| `is_archived` | No. An archived skill cannot be linked to agents, and agents that still link it stop using it. Set `false` to bring it back. |\n\nA PATCH that changes nothing creates no version. Agents that follow the current version pick up the new version on their next turn. Pinned links stay on their version.\n\n## Versions\n\n`GET /skills/{skill_id}/versions` lists versions newest first, without bodies:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"items\": [\n    {\"id\": \"…\", \"skill_id\": \"…\", \"version\": 3, \"title\": \"…\", \"description\": \"…\", \"body_chars\": 412, \"tool_allowlist\": null, \"goal\": null, \"attachment_instruction\": null, \"is_current\": true, \"created_by_user_id\": \"…\", \"created_at\": \"2026-10-05T09:00:00Z\"}\n  ],\n  \"pagination\": {…}\n}\n```\n\n`GET /skills/{skill_id}/versions/{version_id}` adds `body`.\n\n`POST /skills/{skill_id}/versions/{version_id}/restore` copies that version into a **new** current version and returns the skill. History is never rewritten.\n\n## Delete or archive\n\n`DELETE /skills/{skill_id}`:\n\n- A skill that is still linked to an agent or used by a Flow is **archived** instead: `{\"success\": true, \"id\": \"…\", \"action\": \"archived\", \"message\": \"Skill archived because it is still in use\"}`.\n- Otherwise it is deleted with all its versions: `\"action\": \"deleted\"`.\n\n## Usage\n\n`GET /skills/{skill_id}/usage`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"agents\": [{\"id\": \"…\", \"name\": \"Shop Support\", \"mode\": null}],\n  \"flows\": [{\"id\": \"…\", \"name\": \"Returns\", \"status\": \"published\", \"node_ids\": [\"node_7\"]}]\n}\n```\n\n`mode` is the link's override; `null` means the skill's `default_mode`.\n\n## Live sources and web search per skill\n\nWhich [live sources](./turn-lifecycle.md#live-sources) a skill reads and how it overrides the agent's web search mode is set **per agent**, on the agent's skill link (`sources: {web_search_mode, live_source_ids, live_mode, kb_folder_ids, kb_mode}`), not on the skill itself. The same override can point the skill at Knowledge Base folders (`kb_folder_ids`, `kb_mode` `add` or `only`). See [Skill links](./agents-api.md#skill-links). While the skill is loaded, the strongest mode wins and `kb_fallback`/`always` lookups are [enforced](./turn-lifecycle.md#enforced-lookups).\n\n## Goal statistics\n\n`GET /skills/{skill_id}/goal-stats?days=30` (`days` 1-90, default 30):\n\n```json\n{\n  \"days\": 30,\n  \"started\": 120,\n  \"completed\": 87,\n  \"handed_off\": 9,\n  \"completion_rate\": 0.725,\n  \"by_version\": [{\"version\": 3, \"started\": 80, \"completed\": 61, \"handed_off\": 5, \"completion_rate\": 0.7625}],\n  \"truncated\": false\n}\n```\n\nEach goal activation is counted once; test console turns are not counted. `truncated: true` means the period had more activity than the statistics scan, so the numbers are a lower bound. `completion_rate` is `null` when nothing started. See [Skill goals](./skill-goals.md).\n\n## Import skills\n\n`POST /skills/import` creates or updates skills **by slug** in one call. Up to 100 skills. The body can be `{\"skills\": [...]}`, a bare list, or a single skill object.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"skills\": [\n    {\n      \"slug\": \"order-return\",\n      \"title\": \"Order returns\",\n      \"description\": \"Use when the customer wants to return or exchange an item.\",\n      \"mode\": \"model_selected\",\n      \"skill\": \"Ask for the order number and the reason…\",\n      \"tool_allowlist\": null\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n| Field | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `slug` | Optional; derived from `title` when omitted. |\n| `title`, `description` | Required, as on create. |\n| `mode` | The skill's `default_mode`. |\n| `skill` | The body. Required. |\n| `tool_allowlist`, `goal`, `attachment_instruction` | Optional. An omitted `goal` or `attachment_instruction` keeps the existing value on update; `null` clears it. |\n\nEach item is handled on its own. A bad item is reported in `errors` and does not stop the others:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"created\": [{\"index\": 0, \"id\": \"…\", \"slug\": \"order-return\", \"version\": 1}],\n  \"updated\": [],\n  \"unchanged\": [],\n  \"errors\": [{\"index\": 1, \"slug\": \"faq\", \"error\": \"description: Field required\"}]\n}\n```\n\nAn existing skill whose content changed gets a new version (`updated`); identical content is `unchanged`. A slug used twice in the same import is an error for the second item.\n\n## Errors\n\n| Status | When |\n|---|---|\n| `404` | Skill or version not found |\n| `409` | `skill_slug_exists` on create |\n| `422` | Validation errors, including `invalid_goal` (see [Skill goals — Errors](./skill-goals.md#errors)) |\n| `403` | `feature_disabled` or missing scope |\n\n---\n\n# Skill Goals\n\n:::caution Beta\nSkill goals are part of the Conversational Agents Beta.\n\nA **goal** turns a skill into a guided task: the agent collects a defined set of answers, SendSeven validates each answer, saves the valid ones to the contact, and runs your completion actions. Typical uses: qualify a lead, take a callback request, register a return, collect feedback.\n\n- The goal is stored on the **skill version**. Changing it creates a new version, and pinned agents keep their old goal.\n- **Validation is done by SendSeven, not by the model.** The agent submits answers; only answers that pass your rules are accepted and saved. The model never writes contact fields directly.\n- Goals work on every surface: channels, Live Chat, email, Flows and the [test console](./test-console-and-turns.md).\n\n## Add a goal to a skill\n\nSend `goal` when you create or update a skill (or in a [skill import](./skills-api.md#import-skills)):\n\n```json\n{\n  \"slug\": \"qualify-lead\",\n  \"title\": \"Qualify a lead\",\n  \"description\": \"Use when a visitor asks about pricing, a demo or buying for a team.\",\n  \"default_mode\": \"model_selected\",\n  \"body\": \"Be friendly and brief. Ask one question at a time.\",\n  \"goal\": {\n    \"objective\": \"Find out whether the visitor is a good fit for a sales call.\",\n    \"completion_criteria\": \"We know the company size, the budget and the email address.\",\n    \"outputs\": [\n      {\"key\": \"company_size\", \"label\": \"Company size\", \"type\": \"select\", \"options\": [\"1-10\", \"11-50\", \"51-200\", \"200+\"],\n       \"bind\": {\"kind\": \"custom_field\", \"field_id\": \"CUSTOM_FIELD_ID\"}},\n      {\"key\": \"budget\", \"label\": \"Monthly budget (EUR)\", \"type\": \"number\", \"constraints\": {\"min\": 0, \"max\": 1000000}},\n      {\"key\": \"email\", \"bind\": {\"kind\": \"contact_standard\", \"field\": \"email\"}},\n      {\"key\": \"website\", \"type\": \"url\", \"required\": false}\n    ],\n    \"on_max_attempts\": \"handoff\",\n    \"actions\": {\n      \"save_contact_fields\": {\"enabled\": true, \"overwrite_existing\": false},\n      \"assign\": {\"strategy\": \"round_robin_by_tag\", \"tag_id\": \"TAG_ID_SALES\", \"add_tag_ids\": [\"TAG_ID_LEAD\"], \"prefer_online\": true},\n      \"webhook\": {\"enabled\": true},\n      \"after\": {\"type\": \"none\"}\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nSend `\"goal\": null` on `PATCH` to remove the goal; omit it to keep the current one.\n\n## Goal fields\n\n| Field | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `objective` | **Required.** What the agent should achieve. 1-2,000 characters. |\n| `completion_criteria` | Optional. When the goal counts as done. Max 2,000. |\n| `outputs` | Up to 20 answers to collect. Can be empty for an objective-only goal. |\n| `on_max_attempts` | What happens when an answer is still invalid after `max_attempts`: `handoff` (default) hands over to a human, `skip` leaves it empty and moves on. |\n| `actions` | [Completion actions](#completion-actions). |\n\n### Outputs\n\n| Field | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `key` | **Required.** `^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_]{0,63}$`. Unique (case-insensitive). The key in webhooks and Flow variables. |\n| `label` | Max 100. |\n| `description` | Max 500. Help for the agent, for example what to ask. |\n| `required` | Default `true`. |\n| `type` | `text`, `number`, `integer`, `boolean`, `date`, `datetime`, `email`, `phone`, `url`, `select`, `multiselect`. Bound outputs take the type of their field; an unbound output without a type is `text`. |\n| `options` | For `select` and `multiselect`. Up to 100 non-empty strings, max 255 characters each. |\n| `bind` | Where the answer is saved. See [Binding](#binding-to-contact-fields). |\n| `constraints` | Extra validation rules. See [Constraints](#constraints). |\n| `max_attempts` | `1..5`, default `3`. Invalid answers allowed before `on_max_attempts` applies. |\n\n### Binding to contact fields\n\n| `bind` | Saves to |\n|---|---|\n| `{\"kind\": \"contact_standard\", \"field\": \"…\"}` | A standard contact field: `first_name`, `last_name`, `name`, `language` (text), `birthday` (date), `email`, `phone` |\n| `{\"kind\": \"custom_field\", \"field_id\": \"…\"}` | An active [custom field](../../contacts/custom-fields.md) in your workspace |\n\n- A bound output uses the field's type. Declaring a different `type` returns `type_mismatch`.\n- One field can be bound to only one output.\n- A bound `select`/`multiselect` output may restrict `options` to a subset of the field's options.\n- Location and collection custom fields cannot be bound.\n- The custom field's own [validation rules](../../contacts/custom-fields.md#validation-rules-optional) always apply.\n\n### Constraints\n\n| Key | Types |\n|---|---|\n| `min`, `max` | `number`, `integer` |\n| `min_length`, `max_length` (`0..10000`) | `text`, `email`, `phone`, `url` |\n| `pattern` (1-200 characters, a safe regular expression) | `text`, `email`, `phone`, `url` |\n| `date_min`, `date_max` (ISO date or `\"today\"`) | `date`, `datetime` |\n\n`boolean`, `select` and `multiselect` take no constraints. For a bound output, constraints may only **tighten** the field's own rules: a wider range, a longer maximum or a different pattern returns `constraint_loosens`.\n\n## How the agent works with a goal\n\n1. The goal becomes **active** when its skill is loaded (by the classifier, by `load_skill`, as a Flow skill, or as an always-on skill).\n2. The agent asks for the answers and submits them with `submit_goal_fields`. Each answer is normalised and validated; invalid answers come back with an error code (for example `too_small` or `invalid_email`) so the agent can ask again.\n3. The agent finishes with `complete_goal`. This succeeds only when every required output was accepted or skipped. A goal without outputs can be completed after at least 1 contact message following activation, or at least 2 when the goal has completion actions. When every required answer came from the contact record (see [Values on record](#values-on-record)), completion also needs at least 1 contact message after those values were filled in, so the contact can confirm them.\n4. The completing turn has the outcome `goal_completed` (`task_completed` inside a Flow), and the [completion actions](#completion-actions) run.\n\nMore rules:\n\n- **One active goal at a time.** Loading another skill with a goal pauses the current one. Up to 3 paused goals are kept and resume when their skill is loaded again.\n- The active goal's skill stays loaded on the following turns, unless the conversation moves to another topic.\n- Goal tools are free and get 2 extra tool calls on top of `max_tool_calls`.\n- Goal progress is kept per conversation. In the test console it travels in the `state` you send back (key `_agent_goal`).\n\n`complete_goal` errors the agent may receive: `no_active_goal`, `goal_unavailable`, `required_fields_missing` (with `remaining_required`), `too_early`, and for a goal with [`choose_tag`](#choose-tag) `tag_choice_required` and `tag_not_allowed`.\n\n- **Finish with `complete_goal`, not `handoff`.** Tags and assignment of a goal are applied only when the goal completes. When the goal has tags, an assignment strategy or `after.type: \"handoff\"`, the agent is told to finish it with `complete_goal`. Before completion the agent says it has *noted* a value, not that it was saved, because saving can still be refused (for example `conflict`). `set_state` only stores the agent's own notes: it never tags, assigns or changes the conversation or the contact.\n\n### Hand-off while a goal is active\nWhen the agent calls `handoff` while a goal with routing actions (an `assign.strategy` other than `none`, `assign.add_tag_ids`, `choose_tag`, or `after.type: \"handoff\"`) is active:\n\n- **Every required answer is present** (answers from the contact record count), or the goal has no required outputs: the goal is completed and its completion actions run (save, assign and tags, webhook), then the conversation is handed off. The minimum-messages rule of `complete_goal` does not apply. The hand-off replaces the goal's `after` step: `after.type: \"start_flow\"` is **not** started and the turn trace reports it as skipped. A tag the agent passes to `handoff` is added on top of the goal's tags (never twice). Actions still run once per goal activation. The turn outcome stays `handoff`.\n- **Required answers are missing:** the conversation is handed off without completing the goal (no actions run).\n- **Inside a Flow** running the skill's goal: unchanged, the Run AI Assistant node continues on its `escalated` branch and the goal is not completed.\n\nGoals with only `save_contact_fields`, `webhook` or `after.type: \"start_flow\"` are not completed by a hand-off.\n\n## Values on record\nWhen a goal becomes active, outputs bound to the contact's `email`, `phone`, `name`, `first_name`, `last_name` or a custom field are filled from the record of the contact who is chatting, if it has a value. They count as collected, the agent asks the contact to confirm them briefly, and a different answer from the contact replaces them.\n\n| Conversation | Pre-filled |\n|---|---|\n| WhatsApp, SMS, Telegram, Messenger, Instagram | Every bound output with a value (on WhatsApp a missing phone falls back to the number of this chat) |\n| Live Chat with a contact token, a signed identity or a verification in this chat | Only verified values: an email or phone whose contact method is marked verified (or came with a signed identity), and custom values whose source is not `visitor` or `ai` (values the visitor typed or a goal saved are never pre-filled here) |\n| Anonymous Live Chat (cookie only) | Nothing; on the next turn after the visitor verifies |\n| Email | Only an `email` output, with the sender address of the conversation |\n| Test console | Nothing |\n| Other channels | Nothing |\n\n- A stored value that fails the output's validation is not pre-filled.\n- A custom field is pre-filled only when its AI access allows reading it.\n- Saving an unchanged pre-filled value writes nothing; the turn trace reports it as `unchanged`.\n- Pre-fill runs once per goal activation (anonymous Live Chat retries until the visitor is verified).\n\n## Completion actions\n\nActions run **once per goal activation**, in live conversations only, in this order:\n\n| Action | Fields | Effect |\n|---|---|---|\n| `save_contact_fields` | `enabled` (default `true`), `overwrite_existing` (default `false`) | Saves the accepted answers to their bound fields. With `overwrite_existing: false`, a field that already has a value is left alone. |\n| `assign` | `strategy`, `tag_id`, `add_tag_ids` (max 10), `tag_target` (default `conversation`), `prefer_online` (default `true`) | `strategy`: `none` (default), `round_robin_all`, `least_busy_all`, `round_robin_by_tag`, `least_busy_by_tag` (`tag_id` required). Assigns an **unassigned** conversation; an assigned one is never reassigned. `add_tag_ids` are added where `tag_target` says (see [Where tags go](#tag-target)). The `tag_id` of a by-tag strategy always tags the conversation. |\n| `choose_tag` | `tag_ids` (2-10 tags), `max` (1-3, default `1`), `required` (default `true`), `hints` (tag id to text, max 200 characters each), `target` (default `conversation`) | Optional; leave it out or send `null` for none. The agent picks up to `max` of `tag_ids` when it completes the goal, guided by `hints`, and they are added where `target` says, together with `add_tag_ids`. See [Let the agent choose a tag](#choose-tag). |\n| `webhook` | `enabled` (default `true`) | Sends [`agent.goal_completed`](#agentgoal_completed-webhook). |\n| `after` | `type`: `none` (default), `handoff` or `start_flow`; `flow_id`; `variable_prefix` (default `goal`, `^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_]{0,31}$`) | `handoff` hands the conversation to a human (needs escalation on; with a `*_by_tag` strategy the `assign.tag_id` is the hand-off tag). `start_flow` starts a **published, manually startable** Flow for the conversation with the answers in `{{vars.<variable_prefix>.<key>}}`. |\n\n### Where tags go\n`assign.tag_target` (for `add_tag_ids`) and `choose_tag.target` (for the chosen tags) take `conversation` (default), `contact` or `both`. Set them separately, for example a fixed \"Needs callback\" on the conversation and a chosen \"Hot / Warm / Cold\" lead status on the contact. When you send `conversation`, the key is left out of the stored goal.\n\n- A tag on the contact is added exactly like tagging the contact by hand: it sends the `contact.tag_added` webhook and starts automations that react to a contact tag.\n- Tags only ever go to the conversation's own contact. If the conversation has no contact, the contact part is skipped (`no_contact`); with `both`, the conversation part is still added.\n- A tag the agent passes to `handoff` itself always goes on the conversation only.\n\n### Let the agent choose a tag\n`add_tag_ids` are always added. With `choose_tag` the agent decides which tag fits, for example `hot`, `warm` or `cold` for a lead:\n\n```json\n\"choose_tag\": {\n  \"tag_ids\": [\"TAG_ID_HOT\", \"TAG_ID_WARM\", \"TAG_ID_COLD\"],\n  \"max\": 1,\n  \"required\": true,\n  \"hints\": {\"TAG_ID_HOT\": \"Wants to buy within 30 days\", \"TAG_ID_COLD\": \"Only browsing\"}\n}\n```\n\n- `tag_ids` must be existing tags of your workspace (`tag_not_found` otherwise); duplicates are removed. `max` must not be greater than the number of tags. Hints for tags that are not in `tag_ids` are dropped.\n- The agent sees the tags by **name** with their hints and passes its choice by name in the `tag` argument of `complete_goal` (a list when `max` is greater than 1). With `required: true` it cannot complete the goal without a choice (`tag_choice_required`); a name outside the list or more than `max` tags is refused as a whole (`tag_not_allowed`), and the agent can try again. Tags that no longer exist are not offered; when none of the tags exists any more, the goal completes without a chosen tag.\n- When the agent hands off while the goal is active, it can pass its choice in the `goal_tag` argument of `handoff` (separate from the agent's own hand-off `tag`). A missing or invalid choice does not stop the hand-off; the goal is completed without a chosen tag (when every required answer is present, see [Hand-off while a goal is active](#handoff-during-goal)).\n- The `engine_trace` records the choice as reason codes only, never the agent's arguments: a `goal` step with `event: \"tag_chosen\"`, `\"tag_choice_rejected\"` (`reason`: `tag_choice_required` or `tag_not_allowed`) or `\"tag_choice_skipped\"` (`reason`: `tag_choice_missing` or `tag_choice_invalid`).\n- The choice is checked again when the actions run: only tags from `tag_ids` that still exist are added, at most `max`. Tags are never created. A tag that is also in `add_tag_ids`, or that the hand-off already added, is added only once.\n- A goal with `choose_tag` counts as a goal with routing actions (see [Hand-off while a goal is active](#handoff-during-goal)).\n\nInside a Flow, actions run too, but `after` is skipped: the Flow continues on the Run AI Assistant node's `done` branch instead. See [Agents in Flows](./flows.md).\n\nIn the test console nothing is executed. The `engine_trace` contains a `goal_actions_dry_run` step whose `would_execute` lists the fields that would be saved, the assignment, the chosen tag (`choose_tag.chosen_tag_ids`), where tags would go (`assign.tag_targets`, `choose_tag.targets`), the webhook and the `after` action.\n\n## Goal progress in the turn trace\nEach turn (test console response and [turn log](./test-console-and-turns.md#turn-log)) has `goal_progress` for the conversation's current goal, with field **keys only**, never values:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"skill\": \"qualify-lead\", \"skill_title\": \"Qualify lead\", \"status\": \"completed\",\n  \"collected\": [\"email\", \"company_size\"], \"missing\": [], \"prefilled\": [\"email\"],\n  \"actions\": {\n    \"completed_via\": \"handoff\",\n    \"saved\": [{\"key\": \"company_size\", \"label\": \"Company size\", \"field\": \"custom\"}],\n    \"skipped\": [{\"key\": \"email\", \"label\": \"Email\", \"field\": \"email\", \"reason\": \"unchanged\"}],\n    \"tags_added\": [{\"id\": \"…\", \"name\": \"Sales\", \"source\": \"fixed\", \"targets\": [\"conversation\"]}, {\"id\": \"…\", \"name\": \"Hot\", \"source\": \"chosen\", \"targets\": [\"contact\"]}],\n    \"assigned\": true,\n    \"after\": \"handoff\",\n    \"after_skipped\": \"start_flow\"\n  },\n  \"can_complete_now\": null,\n  \"tag_reasons\": [\"tag_choice_required\", \"tag_chosen\"]\n}\n```\n\n- `actions` is set only on the turn whose completion actions ran, otherwise `null`. In the test console it describes what *would* happen and has `\"dry_run\": true`.\n- `completed_via`: `complete_goal` or `handoff` (see [Hand-off while a goal is active](#handoff-during-goal)).\n- `field`: the bound field (`email`, `phone`, `name`, `first_name`, `last_name`, `language`, `birthday`) or `custom`.\n- `skipped[].reason`: `conflict` (the email or phone already belongs to another contact), `unchanged`, `already_has_value` (with `overwrite_existing: false`), `invalid`, `no_contact` or `error`.\n- `after`: `none`, `handoff` or `start_flow`; `null` inside a Flow (the Flow continues on `done`). `after_skipped` is `start_flow` when a hand-off replaced it.\n- `tag_reasons` (only for a goal with [`choose_tag`](#choose-tag), only on a turn where something happened; absent otherwise): up to 4 codes for this turn. `tag_chosen` means a choice was accepted; `tag_choice_required` and `tag_not_allowed` mean a `complete_goal` call was refused; `tag_choice_missing` and `tag_choice_invalid` mean a hand-off or completion went ahead without a chosen tag. Codes only, never the agent's arguments.\n- `tags_added[].targets`: where the tag was added (`conversation`, `contact` or both). When the contact part is skipped, `reason` is `no_contact` (the conversation has no contact) or `error`; a contact-only tag that was not added anywhere is listed with `targets: []`.\n- `tags_added[].name` is `null` when the tag no longer exists. `tags_added[].source`: `fixed` (`assign.tag_id` or `add_tag_ids`), `chosen` (picked by the agent for `choose_tag`) or `handoff` (passed with the hand-off).\n\n## `agent.goal_completed` webhook\n\nSubscribe your webhook endpoint to `agent.goal_completed` (see [Webhook setup](../../webhooks/setup.md)). The event uses the standard envelope:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"evt_4f9c2a7b1d3e5f60\",\n  \"type\": \"agent.goal_completed\",\n  \"created_at\": \"2026-10-05T10:00:01Z\",\n  \"tenant_id\": \"…\",\n  \"event_id\": \"6f1c1d1e-2b3a-4c5d-8e9f-0a1b2c3d4e5f\",\n  \"data\": {\n    \"agent\": {\"id\": \"…\", \"name\": \"Sales Assistant\"},\n    \"skill\": {\"id\": \"…\", \"slug\": \"qualify-lead\", \"version\": 3},\n    \"conversation_id\": \"…\",\n    \"contact_id\": \"…\",\n    \"channel_type\": \"whatsapp\",\n    \"outputs\": {\"company_size\": \"11-50\", \"budget\": 5000, \"email\": \"jane@example.com\", \"website\": null},\n    \"saved_fields\": [\"company_size\", \"email\"],\n    \"completed_at\": \"2026-10-05T10:00:00Z\",\n    \"flow_run_id\": null\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- `event_id` identifies the goal activation and is the same on every delivery attempt. Use it to de-duplicate.\n- `outputs` has at most 20 keys. Skipped optional answers are `null`. Text is cut at 4,000 characters and lists at 50 items.\n- Full reference: [Webhook events — agent.goal_completed](../../webhooks/events.md#agentgoal_completed).\n\n## Statistics\n\n`GET /automation/skills/{skill_id}/goal-stats?days=30` returns started, completed and handed-off goals, the completion rate and a per-version breakdown. See [Skills API — Goal statistics](./skills-api.md#goal-statistics).\n\n## Errors\n\nAn invalid goal is rejected with `422`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"detail\": {\n    \"code\": \"invalid_goal\",\n    \"message\": \"The skill goal is invalid\",\n    \"errors\": [\n      {\"path\": \"outputs[1].constraints.max\", \"code\": \"constraint_loosens\", \"message\": \"…\"}\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nUp to 50 errors are returned, each with the `path` of the problem.\n\n| `code` | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `invalid`, `invalid_type`, `invalid_value` | Malformed goal, wrong type or value |\n| `options_required`, `duplicate_option`, `options_not_applicable`, `options_not_subset` | Problems with `options` |\n| `constraint_not_applicable`, `min_greater_than_max`, `constraint_loosens` | Problems with `constraints` |\n| `unknown_standard_field`, `type_mismatch`, `duplicate_bind`, `unsupported_field_type` | Problems with `bind` |\n| `custom_field_not_found`, `custom_field_inactive` | The bound custom field does not exist or is inactive |\n| `tag_not_found` | `assign.tag_id`, an `add_tag_ids` entry or a `choose_tag.tag_ids` entry is not in your workspace |\n| `flow_not_found`, `flow_not_published`, `flow_not_manually_startable` | Problems with `after.flow_id` |\n\nAnswer validation codes the agent can receive: `required`, `not_in_options`, `not_a_number`, `not_an_integer`, `too_small`, `too_large`, `too_short`, `too_long`, `pattern_mismatch`, `invalid_email`, `invalid_phone`, `invalid_url`, `invalid_date`, `date_too_early`, `date_too_late`, `invalid_boolean`, `invalid_language`, `unsupported`."
  },
  "agent-fields": {
    "title": "Agent field reference",
    "description": "Every field of a Conversational Agent (persona, instructions, model tier, tools, hand-off, language, escalation, skills, knowledge sources), plus the draft and publish rules and the readiness check.",
    "text": "# Agents API\n\n:::caution Beta\nConversational Agents are in Beta and available on Professional, Scale and Enterprise. On other plans every endpoint returns `403 {\"detail\": {\"code\": \"feature_disabled\", \"feature\": \"ai_agents\"}}`.\n\nBase URL: `https://api.sendseven.com/api/v1/automation`\n\n| Method | Path | Scope | Success |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `GET` | `/agents` | `automation:read` | `200` paginated list |\n| `POST` | `/agents` | `automation:create` | `201` agent |\n| `GET` | `/agents/{agent_id}` | `automation:read` | `200` agent |\n| `PATCH` | `/agents/{agent_id}` | `automation:update` | `200` agent |\n| `DELETE` | `/agents/{agent_id}` | `automation:delete` | `200` `{success, id, message}` |\n| `PUT` | `/agents/{agent_id}/skills` | `automation:update` | `200` agent |\n| `GET` / `PUT` / `DELETE` | `/agents/{agent_id}/draft` | `automation:read` / `automation:update` | `200` draft — see [Drafts & publishing](./drafts-and-publishing.md) |\n| `POST` | `/agents/{agent_id}/publish` | `automation:publish` | `200` agent — see [Who can publish](./drafts-and-publishing.md#who-can-publish) |\n| `POST` | `/agents/{agent_id}/avatar` | `automation:update` | `200` draft — see [Avatar](./drafts-and-publishing.md#avatar) |\n| `GET` | `/agents/insights/overview?days=30` | `automation:read` | `200` — see [Insights overview](./drafts-and-publishing.md#insights-overview) |\n| `GET` | `/agents/{agent_id}/knowledge-sources` | `automation:read` | `200` list |\n| `POST` | `/agents/{agent_id}/knowledge-sources` | `automation:update` | `201` source |\n| `PATCH` | `/agents/{agent_id}/knowledge-sources/{source_id}` | `automation:update` | `200` source |\n| `DELETE` | `/agents/{agent_id}/knowledge-sources/{source_id}` | `automation:update` | `200` `{success, id, message}` |\n| `GET` / `POST` / `PATCH` / `PUT` / `DELETE` | `/lookup-tables…` | `automation:*` | see [Lookup tables](#lookup-tables-api) |\n| `POST` | `/agents/{agent_id}/test` | `automation:update` | `200` — see [Test console](./test-console-and-turns.md) |\n| `GET` | `/agents/{agent_id}/turns` | `automation:read` | `200` — see [Turn log](./test-console-and-turns.md#turn-log) |\n| `GET` | `/agents/{agent_id}/feedback` | `automation:read` | `200` — see [Answer ratings & feedback](./test-console-and-turns.md#answer-ratings) |\n| `GET` | `/agents/{agent_id}/skill-suggestions` | `automation:read` | `200` — see [Skill suggestions](./feedback-loop.md#skill-suggestions) |\n| `POST` | `/agents/{agent_id}/skill-suggestions/generate` | `automation:update` | `201` suggestion |\n| `POST` | `/agents/{agent_id}/skill-suggestions/{suggestion_id}/approve` | `automation:update` | `200` `{suggestion, skill_version}` |\n| `POST` | `/agents/{agent_id}/skill-suggestions/{suggestion_id}/dismiss` | `automation:update` | `200` suggestion |\n| `GET` | `/hub/stats?days=30` | `automation:read` | `200` — see [Agents overview](#agents-overview) |\n| `POST` | `/bots/{bot_id}/duplicate` | `automation:create` | `201` bot — see [Duplicate an agent](#duplicate-an-agent) |\n| `GET` | `/hub/templates` | `automation:read` | `200` `{items}` — see [Templates](#templates) |\n| `POST` | `/hub/templates/{key}/create` | `automation:create` | `201` `{agent_id, bot_id}` |\n\n## List agents\n\n`GET /agents?page=1&page_size=50&search=support`\n\n| Parameter | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `page` | Default `1` |\n| `page_size` | Default `50`, max `100` |\n| `search` | Optional, max 100 characters, matches the name |\n\nReturns `{\"items\": [Agent, …], \"pagination\": {…}}`.\n\n## Create an agent\n\n`POST /agents` — unknown fields are rejected with `422`.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"Shop Support\",\n  \"is_active\": false,\n  \"persona\": \"You are Mia, the friendly support assistant of Example Shop.\",\n  \"instructions\": \"Answer questions about orders, shipping and returns.\",\n  \"model_tier\": \"standard\",\n  \"display_name\": \"Mia\",\n  \"greeting_message\": \"Hi! I'm Mia. How can I help?\",\n  \"language_settings\": {\"base_language\": \"en\", \"response_language\": null},\n  \"escalation\": {\"enabled\": true, \"handoff_message\": \"I'm connecting you with a colleague.\"},\n  \"tools_config\": {\"search_knowledge\": true, \"web_search\": false, \"handoff\": true, \"set_state\": true},\n  \"handoff_config\": {\"keywords\": [\"human\"], \"max_turns\": 30, \"max_consecutive_fallbacks\": 2, \"handoff_tag_ids\": [\"TAG_ID\"], \"handoff_tag_hints\": {\"TAG_ID\": \"billing questions, invoices, refunds\"}},\n  \"skills\": [{\"skill_id\": \"SKILL_ID\", \"mode\": null, \"pinned_version_id\": null}],\n  \"knowledge_sources\": [{\"source_type\": \"faq\"}]\n}\n```\n\n### Fields\n\n| Field | Type | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `name` | string | **Required** on create. 1-255 characters. |\n| `is_active` | boolean | Default `true`. Create agents inactive, test them, then activate. Switching a paused agent on needs `automation:publish`. |\n| `publish` | boolean | Create only. Default `true` (needs `automation:publish`, see [Who can publish](./drafts-and-publishing.md#who-can-publish)). `false` creates a never-published agent (`publish_state: \"draft\"`) that does not answer contacts until you [publish](./drafts-and-publishing.md#publish) it. |\n| `persona` | string | Who the agent is and how it speaks. Max 20,000 characters. |\n| `instructions` | string | What the agent does. Max 20,000 characters. |\n| `model_tier` | `standard` \\| `advanced` | Default `standard` (\"Fast\", typically about 1 to 2 AI credits per reply). `advanced` (\"Advanced\") costs about 3.3 times as much for the same work (typically about 3 to 8 per reply) and always uses a 15-second turn budget (`turn_timeout_ms` is ignored). Web search credits are the same. |\n| `display_name` | string | Name shown to contacts. Max 255. |\n| `greeting_message` | string | Max 2,000. Blank clears it. |\n| `ai_disclosure_message` | string | The notice that the contact is talking to an AI. Max 2,000. It cannot be removed: blank or `null` uses the built-in, localized default. |\n| `ai_disclosure_include_escalation` | boolean | Also tell the contact how to reach a human. Default `true`. |\n| `language_settings` | object | See [Language settings](#language-settings). |\n| `escalation` | object | See [Escalation settings](#escalation-settings). |\n| `tools_config` | object | See [Tools](#tools-config). |\n| `handoff_config` | object | See [Hand-off config](#handoff-config). |\n| `max_tool_calls` | integer | Tool calls allowed per turn. Default `4`. Values outside `1..6` are clamped, not rejected. |\n| `turn_timeout_ms` | integer | Legacy automatic time budget per turn for `model_tier: standard`, used only while `turn_time_limits.default` is not set. Default `8000`. Values outside `2000..15000` are clamped. `advanced` agents always get 15000. |\n| `turn_time_limits` | object | Response time limit per turn, in seconds. See [Turn time limits](#turn-time-limits). |\n| `add_detected_languages` | boolean | Add languages the agent detects from the contact to the contact's `languages` list. Default `true`. See [Add detected languages to the contact](#add-detected-languages-to-the-contact). |\n| `email_settings` | object | How the agent answers emails. See [Email settings](#email-settings). |\n| `source_footer_enabled` | boolean | Show a \"Sources\" footer under answers based on your knowledge. Default `false`. |\n| `source_footer_mode` | `urls_only` \\| `urls_and_kb` | Default `urls_only` (links to your website pages only). `urls_and_kb` also adds a generic \"Knowledge Base\" line for FAQ and document sources. |\n| `source_link_params` | object | Query parameters added to your website links in the footer, per channel. See [Source link parameters](#source-link-parameters). |\n| `settings` | object | Free-form settings, max 16 KB, max 6 levels deep. |\n| `skills` | array | Create only. Up to 50 [skill links](#skill-links). |\n| `knowledge_sources` | array | Create only. Up to 20 [knowledge sources](#knowledge-sources). |\n\n`PATCH /agents/{agent_id}` accepts the same fields except `skills` and `knowledge_sources`, all optional. Use `PUT /agents/{agent_id}/skills` and the knowledge source endpoints for those.\n\n### Language settings\n\n| Field | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `base_language` | Max 10 characters. The agent's main language. Response default `en`. |\n| `response_language` | `null` or `auto` = answer in the contact's language. A language code = always answer in that language. |\n| `language_detection_strategy` | `cascade` (default behaviour) or `fixed` (always answer in `base_language`). |\n| `auto_translate_messages` | boolean |\n\n`PATCH` updates only the language fields you send; omitted fields keep their value.\n\n**Auto vs fixed.** These are the two language modes the app and the [agent builder](./agent-builder.md) use:\n\n| Mode | `language_detection_strategy` | `response_language` | The agent answers in |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Auto | `cascade` | `null` | The contact's language |\n| Fixed | `fixed` | the `base_language` code | `base_language` |\n\nTo switch an agent from fixed to auto, send **both** fields. If you only change the strategy, the old `response_language` stays set and the agent keeps answering in that language:\n\n```json\nPATCH /agents/{agent_id}\n{\"language_settings\": {\"language_detection_strategy\": \"cascade\", \"response_language\": null}}\n```\n\nTo switch to fixed, send `{\"language_detection_strategy\": \"fixed\", \"response_language\": \"<base_language>\"}`.\n\n### Add detected languages to the contact\n\n`add_detected_languages` (boolean, default `true`, also for existing agents) lets the agent remember the languages a contact writes in. When the agent detects the contact's language for the first time in a conversation, or switches because the contact writes in a different language, it appends that code to the contact's `languages` list. Example: a contact with preferred language `de` writes in Turkish and the agent switches to `tr`; the contact's `languages` becomes `de,tr`.\n\n- The contact's preferred `language` never changes, and neither does the language the agent answers in.\n- Only languages detected from the contact count. A fallback to `base_language` or `language_detection_strategy: \"fixed\"` never adds a code.\n- A code the contact already has (as `language` or in `languages`) is not added again. Each added code updates the contact and fires the `contact.updated` webhook.\n\nSend it as a top-level `add_detected_languages` field or inside `settings.add_detected_languages` (the top-level field wins). On `PATCH`, omitted or `null` keeps the current value. In the [draft document](./drafts-and-publishing.md) it is saved with the other agent settings and goes live when you publish. Responses always return a boolean.\n\n### Escalation settings\n\nSame names and meaning as on the [FAQ Bot](../build-an-assistant.md):\n\n| Field | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `enabled` | Whether the agent may hand over to a human. With `false`, the `handoff` tool is not offered and the deterministic triggers do not fire. |\n| `offers_escalation` | boolean |\n| `handoff_message` | Sent to the contact on hand-over. Max 2,000. |\n| `escalation_keywords` | Used when `handoff_config.keywords` is not set. |\n| `offline_escalation_behavior` | Live chat: what happens when nobody is online. |\n| `offline_message`, `outside_hours_message` | Max 2,000 each. |\n| `is_escalation_schedule_enabled`, `escalation_timezone`, `escalation_schedule` | The agent's own hand-over schedule (used in `custom` mode). |\n| `escalation_schedule_mode` | `\"workspace\"` (follow the workspace business hours) or `\"custom\"` (own schedule). Always set in responses; see [Team availability](./handoff-and-assignment.md#team-availability). |\n\nRouting a hand-over to a team member is configured on the agent's bot record. See [Hand-off & assignment](./handoff-and-assignment.md).\n\n### Tools config\n| Key | Default | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `search_knowledge` | `true` | |\n| `web_search` | `false` | Also needs an enabled `web_search` knowledge source. Set it explicitly. |\n| `web_search_mode` | `model` when `web_search` is on, else `off` | When the agent searches the web: `off`, `model` (the agent decides, using the `web_search` tool), `kb_fallback` (every `search_knowledge` call that finds nothing also searches the web with the same query) or `always` (the first `search_knowledge` call of each reply also searches the web). Setting a mode also sets `web_search` (`off` → `false`, anything else → `true`); setting `web_search` to `false` sets the mode to `off`. Agents created before this setting existed keep `model`. `GET` always returns the effective mode. See [Web search modes](./turn-lifecycle.md#web-search-modes). |\n| `live_mode` | `model` | When the agent reads its live sources (`live_source` knowledge sources with `scope: \"agent\"`): `off`, `model` (the agent decides, using the `read_live_source` tool), `kb_fallback` (read when the Knowledge Base finds nothing) or `always` (read on every reply). Send `null` to go back to the default. `GET` always returns the effective mode. |\n| `web_cache_ttl_minutes` | not set | 10-60. How long identical web searches of this agent are reused. Send `null` to remove it. |\n| `handoff` | `true` | Only offered while escalation is enabled. |\n| `set_state` | `true` | |\n| `load_skill` | `true` | |\n| `read_images` | `false` | Beta. Read images the contact sends. The agent opens a file only when the conversation needs it; reading it is part of the reply's AI work (typically about 0.5 AI credits per image on Fast, about 0.8 on Advanced). Also sets the bot record's `vision_enabled`. |\n| `read_documents` | `false` | Beta. Read PDF documents the contact sends, charged like images (part of the reply's AI work, typically about 0.8 AI credits per PDF on Fast, about 1.4 on Advanced). |\n| `read_voice_messages` | `false` | Beta. Transcribe the **voice notes** the contact sends and answer the text as if it had been typed. Audio files that are not voice notes are not transcribed. Charged like images (part of the reply's AI work). When off, the agent replies asking the contact to write their message instead. Up to 3 voice notes per reply, each up to 5 minutes and 20 MB. The transcription model follows the workspace **Voice message transcription** level, not `model_tier`. See [Voice messages](./turn-lifecycle.md#voice-messages). |\n| `max_images_per_message` | `3` | 1-3. Further images in the same message are skipped. |\n| `max_attachments_per_conversation` | `10` | 1-50. Images, PDFs and voice notes **read** in one conversation; after that, files are skipped. |\n\nUnknown keys are rejected. `complete_task` is not configurable: it exists only when a Flow gives the agent a task. Details in [Tools & turn lifecycle](./turn-lifecycle.md#tools).\n\nOn `PATCH`, `tools_config` is **merged** into the stored settings: send only the keys you want to change. Responses always contain every key with its effective value. Turn image reading on or off with `read_images`: it also updates the bot record's `vision_enabled`. An agent created before `read_images` existed reports its bot's `vision_enabled` until you set `read_images`. Limits and behaviour: [Images and PDFs](./turn-lifecycle.md#images-and-pdfs).\n\n### Hand-off config\n| Field | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `keywords` | Up to 50 words (max 100 characters each, de-duplicated case-insensitively). A contact message containing one hands over immediately, before any AI call. When not set, the escalation keywords are used. |\n| `max_turns` | `1..200`. Hands over once the conversation goes past this many contact turns. |\n| `max_consecutive_fallbacks` | `1..20`, default `2`. Hands over after this many turns in a row in which the agent could not answer. |\n| `handoff_tag_ids` | Up to 20 tag ids from your workspace. The agent may pick one of them when it hands over, and the tag is added to the conversation. Unknown tags: `422 {\"code\": \"invalid_handoff_tags\", \"message\": \"Unknown handoff tag(s)\", \"tag_ids\": […]}`. |\n| `handoff_tag_hints` | Optional object `{tag_id: \"when to use\"}`, shown to the agent next to each tag. Text is trimmed, empty hints are dropped, each hint is at most 200 characters (longer: `422`), at most 20 entries. Hints for tag ids not in `handoff_tag_ids` are dropped on save. Default `{}`. |\n| `handoff_message` | Max 2,000. Message sent on hand-over. |\n\n`handoff_config` may be at most 8 KB serialized.\n\n### Turn time limits\n`turn_time_limits` sets how long one turn may take before the agent answers with what it has, per channel group:\n\n| Key | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `default` | Every channel. `null` means the legacy automatic budget (`turn_timeout_ms`, 15 seconds for `advanced`). |\n| `live_chat` | Live Chat override. `null` means the same as `default`. |\n| `email` | Email override. `null` means the same as `default`. |\n\n- Allowed values are `30` and `60` seconds (also listed in the response as `turn_time_limit_options`). 60 seconds is the maximum: a higher value returns `422`. A limit above 60 seconds saved earlier is returned and applied as `60`, and stored as `60` the next time the agent is saved.\n- New agents start with `{\"default\": 30}`. Agents created before this setting existed keep the automatic budget until you set `default`.\n- Overrides apply only while `default` is set. When it is set, the limit is the whole turn budget for both model tiers and `turn_timeout_ms` is ignored.\n- Send it as a top-level `turn_time_limits` field or inside `settings.turn_time_limits` (the top-level field wins). Both are partial: omitted keys keep their value and `null` clears a key.\n- Only the keys `default`, `live_chat` and `email` are accepted. Another key or value returns `422` with code `invalid_turn_time_limit`.\n- The response always contains all three keys.\n\n### Email settings\n`email_settings` controls how the agent answers emails:\n\n| Key | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `reply_mode` | `draft` (default): every reply is saved as a draft for your team. `auto`: a reply whose confidence reaches `confidence_threshold` is sent, anything below becomes a draft. |\n| `confidence_threshold` | Integer `0` to `100`. Default `80`. |\n| `triage_skill_id` | A skill of this workspace that decides which emails get a reply at all, see [Email triage skills](./email-triage-skills.md). `null` (default) uses the built-in triage and clears a set skill. |\n\n- Send it as a top-level `email_settings` field or inside `settings.email_settings` (the top-level field wins). Both are partial: omitted keys keep their value.\n- A wrong type, a value out of range or an unknown key returns `422` with code `email_settings.invalid`. A `triage_skill_id` that is not a skill of this workspace returns `422` `email_settings.invalid_skill`.\n- Even with `reply_mode: \"auto\"`, a reply is saved as a draft when it is not backed by your knowledge, skills or verified contact data, when the sender could not be verified, or when a check could not run.\n- Contact-form emails: when a website contact form sends the email and names the customer in Reply-To, the agent treats that address as the sender and always saves its reply as a draft, also with `reply_mode: \"auto\"`.\n\n### Source link parameters\n\nAdd tracking parameters to links to **your website pages** in the sources footer. Keys are channel types, or `default`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"source_link_params\": {\n    \"default\": {\"utm_source\": \"sendseven\", \"utm_medium\": \"{channel_type}\"},\n    \"live_chat\": {\"utm_source\": \"chat\", \"utm_content\": \"{widget_id}\"}\n  }\n}\n```\n\nPlaceholders: `{bot_id}`, `{bot_name}` (as a URL slug), `{channel_type}`, `{channel_id}`, `{tenant_id}`, `{widget_id}` (Live Chat only). A parameter whose placeholder cannot be filled for a message is left out.\n\n### Response\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"…\",\n  \"bot_id\": \"…\",\n  \"tenant_id\": \"…\",\n  \"name\": \"Shop Support\",\n  \"is_active\": false,\n  \"persona\": \"…\",\n  \"display_name\": \"Mia\",\n  \"avatar_url\": null,\n  \"greeting_message\": \"…\",\n  \"ai_disclosure_message\": null,\n  \"ai_disclosure_include_escalation\": true,\n  \"instructions\": \"…\",\n  \"model_tier\": \"standard\",\n  \"language_settings\": {\"response_language\": null, \"base_language\": \"en\", \"auto_translate_messages\": false, \"language_detection_strategy\": null},\n  \"escalation\": {\"enabled\": true, \"offers_escalation\": true, \"handoff_message\": \"…\", \"escalation_keywords\": null, \"offline_escalation_behavior\": \"leave_message\", \"offline_message\": null, \"outside_hours_message\": null, \"is_escalation_schedule_enabled\": false, \"escalation_timezone\": \"UTC\", \"escalation_schedule\": null, \"escalation_schedule_mode\": \"workspace\"},\n  \"source_footer_enabled\": false,\n  \"source_footer_mode\": \"urls_only\",\n  \"source_link_params\": null,\n  \"tools_config\": {\"search_knowledge\": true, \"web_search\": false, \"handoff\": true, \"set_state\": true, \"load_skill\": true, \"read_images\": false, \"read_documents\": false, \"read_voice_messages\": false, \"max_images_per_message\": 3, \"max_attachments_per_conversation\": 10},\n  \"handoff_config\": {\"keywords\": [\"human\"], \"max_turns\": 30, \"max_consecutive_fallbacks\": null, \"handoff_tag_ids\": [], \"handoff_message\": null},\n  \"max_tool_calls\": 4,\n  \"turn_timeout_ms\": 8000,\n  \"turn_time_limits\": {\"default\": 30, \"live_chat\": null, \"email\": 60},\n  \"turn_time_limit_options\": [30, 60],\n  \"add_detected_languages\": true,\n  \"email_settings\": {\"reply_mode\": \"draft\", \"confidence_threshold\": 80, \"triage_skill_id\": null},\n  \"settings\": null,\n  \"skills\": [\n    {\"skill_id\": \"…\", \"slug\": \"order-return\", \"title\": \"Order returns\", \"mode\": null, \"effective_mode\": \"model_selected\", \"pinned_version_id\": null, \"current_version\": 3, \"pinned_version\": null, \"is_archived\": false, \"sort_order\": 0}\n  ],\n  \"knowledge_sources\": [\n    {\"id\": \"…\", \"source_type\": \"faq\", \"kb_folder_id\": null, \"kb_folder_name\": null, \"allowed_domains\": null, \"is_enabled\": true, \"config\": null, \"sort_order\": 0, \"created_at\": \"2026-10-05T09:00:00Z\", \"updated_at\": \"2026-10-05T09:00:00Z\"}\n  ],\n  \"publish_state\": \"published\",\n  \"has_unpublished_changes\": false,\n  \"draft_updated_at\": null,\n  \"published_at\": \"2026-10-05T09:00:00Z\",\n  \"created_at\": \"2026-10-05T09:00:00Z\",\n  \"updated_at\": \"2026-10-05T09:00:00Z\"\n}\n```\n\n`publish_state`, `has_unpublished_changes`, `draft_updated_at` and `published_at` are explained in [Drafts & publishing](./drafts-and-publishing.md#publish-state). The fields above always show the **live** configuration.\n\n## Delete an agent\n\n`DELETE /agents/{agent_id}` deletes the agent with its bot record, channel rules, sessions, skill links and knowledge sources. Skills themselves are kept.\n\nWhile a published or paused Flow uses the agent, the delete is refused:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"detail\": {\n    \"code\": \"agent_in_use\",\n    \"message\": \"This agent is used by published or paused flows. Remove it from these flows first.\",\n    \"flows\": [{\"id\": \"…\", \"name\": \"Returns\", \"status\": \"published\", \"node_ids\": [\"node_7\"]}]\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Duplicate an agent\n\n`POST /bots/{bot_id}/duplicate` (`automation:create`) copies a Conversational Agent inside the same workspace and returns the new bot (`201`, same shape as `GET /bots/{bot_id}`).\n\n```json\n{\"name\": \"Support (EN)\"}\n```\n\n`name` is optional. Without it the copy is called `<name> (Kopie)` in German workspaces and `<name> (copy)` otherwise.\n\n| Copied | Not copied |\n|---|---|\n| Persona, instructions, greeting, language, escalation and tool settings | Channel rules (the copy is not connected to any channel) |\n| Skill links incl. mode and pinned versions (the skills are shared, not cloned) | Conversations, sessions, replies, ratings, feedback, skill suggestions |\n| Knowledge sources: Knowledge Base folders, FAQ, web search domains, live sources | Usage counters and history |\n\nThe copy is **paused** (`is_active: false`) and placed **last** in the bot order. Only Conversational Agents can be duplicated: other bot types return `422 {\"detail\": {\"code\": \"duplicate_classic_not_supported\"}}`. A bot from another workspace returns `404`.\n\n## Templates\n\n`GET /hub/templates` (`automation:read`) lists the ready-made agent templates in a fixed order:\n\n```json\n{\"items\": [{\"key\": \"product_faq\", \"has_goal\": false, \"suggested_channels\": [\"whatsapp\", \"live_chat\", \"email\"], \"skills\": [\"answer-product-questions\"]}]}\n```\n\nKeys: `product_faq`, `customer_support`, `lead_qualification`, `appointment_booking`, `email_reply`, `feedback_nps`. `has_goal` is `true` when a template skill collects data with a [goal](./skill-goals.md). Titles and descriptions are not returned; show your own copy per key.\n\n`POST /hub/templates/{key}/create` (`automation:create`) creates a **paused** agent with persona, greeting, hand-off message, skills and knowledge sources (Knowledge Base + FAQ) from the template, written in your workspace language (German or English; other languages get English). Returns `201 {\"agent_id\": \"…\", \"bot_id\": \"…\"}`. No AI credits are used. Unknown key: `404 template_not_found`. Duplicate and template create use the same plan gate as `POST /agents`.\n\n## Agents overview\n`GET /hub/stats?days=30` (`automation:read`) returns KPIs and one row per bot of the workspace (Conversational Agents and classic bots, paused ones included), in the same order as `PUT /bots/reorder`. `days` is `7`, `30` (default) or `90` calendar days in your workspace timezone, today included; any other value returns `422`.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"range_days\": 30, \"period_start\": \"2026-09-06T22:00:00Z\", \"period_end\": \"2026-10-06T21:59:59Z\",\n  \"series_dates\": [\"2026-09-07\", \"…\", \"2026-10-06\"], \"min_sample\": 20, \"truncated\": false,\n  \"totals\": {\"conversations\": 808, \"conversations_prev\": 721, \"conversations_delta_pct\": 12.07,\n             \"resolved\": 513, \"resolved_pct\": 63.5, \"handoffs\": 295, \"handoff_pct\": 36.5,\n             \"insufficient_data\": false, \"credits\": 1412, \"credits_eur\": 14.12},\n  \"agents\": [{\n    \"bot_id\": \"BOT_ID\", \"agent_id\": \"AGENT_ID\", \"name\": \"Support Agent\", \"bot_type\": \"agent\", \"kind\": \"agent\",\n    \"subtitle\": {\"type\": \"goal\", \"label\": \"Book a demo\"}, \"status\": \"live\", \"has_unpublished_changes\": false,\n    \"priority\": 3, \"avatar_url\": null,\n    \"channels\": [{\"channel_id\": \"CHANNEL_ID\", \"channel_type\": \"whatsapp\", \"name\": \"WA Support\"}],\n    \"skills_count\": 5, \"conversations\": 120, \"conversations_prev\": 98, \"resolved\": 98, \"resolved_pct\": 81.7,\n    \"handoffs\": 22, \"handoff_pct\": 18.3, \"insufficient_data\": false, \"credits\": 310,\n    \"sparkline\": [0, 3, 5], \"updated_at\": \"2026-10-04T10:12:00Z\", \"updated_by_name\": null\n  }]\n}\n```\n\n**Definitions** (shared with `GET /analytics`, `GET /bots/{bot_id}/analytics` and [agent analytics](#analytics), so the numbers always match):\n\n- **Conversation:** a conversation the bot replied in during the period, counted once per bot, dated by its first reply. Test-console replies are not counted.\n- **Handoff:** a conversation in which the bot handed over to your team. **Resolved** = conversations − handoffs. Both percentages are always between 0 and 100.\n- `resolved_pct` / `handoff_pct` per bot are `null` (and `insufficient_data: true`) below `min_sample` (20) conversations. In `totals` they are `null` only without conversations; a conversation several bots replied in counts once there.\n- `conversations_prev` is the same-length window before; `conversations_delta_pct` is `null` when it is 0.\n- `credits` are the AI credits Conversational Agents used (classic bots show `0`); `credits_eur` uses your plan's AI credit price.\n- `status`: `paused` = switched off; `not_connected` = switched on, but no active channel rule (or published Flow) can route a message to it; otherwise `live`.\n- `subtitle.type`: `goal` (label = title of the first skill with a goal), `template` (label = template key) or `none`.\n\n## Skill links\n\n`PUT /agents/{agent_id}/skills` replaces **all** of the agent's skill links. Send an empty list to unlink every skill.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"skills\": [\n    {\"skill_id\": \"SKILL_ID_BASICS\", \"mode\": \"always_on\"},\n    {\"skill_id\": \"SKILL_ID_RETURNS\", \"pinned_version_id\": \"VERSION_ID\", \"sort_order\": 1}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n| Field | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `skill_id` | **Required.** A skill in your workspace. |\n| `mode` | `always_on`, `model_selected`, `flow_only` or `null` (use the skill's `default_mode`). |\n| `pinned_version_id` | `null` = follow the skill's current version. Otherwise a version of **this** skill; the agent keeps using it when the skill changes. |\n| `sort_order` | `0..10000` |\n| `sources` | Optional per-skill source override (see below). Omit it to keep the stored override; send `null` to remove it. |\n\n`sources` changes the agent's sources while this skill is loaded:\n\n```json\n{\"skill_id\": \"SKILL_ID_PRICES\", \"sources\": {\"web_search_mode\": \"off\", \"live_source_ids\": [\"LIVE_SOURCE_ID\"], \"live_mode\": \"always\", \"kb_folder_ids\": [\"KB_FOLDER_ID\"], \"kb_mode\": \"add\"}}\n```\n\n| Key | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `web_search_mode` | `off`, `model`, `kb_fallback`, `always` or `null` (use the agent's mode). |\n| `live_source_ids` | Up to 5 ids of this agent's `live_source` knowledge sources. Any other id returns `422 {\"code\": \"invalid_skill_sources\"}`. |\n| `live_mode` | `model`, `kb_fallback`, `always` or `null` (use the agent's `live_mode`). |\n| `kb_folder_ids` | Up to 10 Knowledge Base folder ids searched while this skill is loaded, subfolders included. Any folder of your workspace works (also one the agent has no source for), except the system folders for conversation history and ticket summaries. An unknown id returns `422 {\"code\": \"invalid_skill_sources\"}`. |\n| `kb_mode` | `add` (default, also for `null`): the agent's own Knowledge Base sources **plus** these folders. `only`: only these folders while the skill is loaded. `only` needs at least one folder (`422` otherwise); without folders `kb_mode` is ignored. |\n\n**Knowledge Base scope when several skills are loaded.** One reply can load several skills, so SendSeven searches the union of what they allow:\n\n- The folders of every loaded skill that has `kb_folder_ids` are searched (`add` and `only` alike).\n- The agent's own Knowledge Base sources (its `kb_all`, `kb_folder` and `faq` sources, or the whole Knowledge Base when it has none) are searched too, **unless** every loaded skill with a Knowledge Base override is `only`. A single loaded skill with `add`, or with no Knowledge Base override at all, brings them back.\n- Skills with mode `always_on` that have no Knowledge Base override do not count: they are loaded on every reply, so they would otherwise cancel `only` everywhere. An `always_on` skill **with** `kb_mode: \"only\"` narrows every reply unless another loaded skill says `add`.\n- Conversation-history and ticket-summary folders are never searched, also not through a skill.\n\nThis is relevance scoping, not access control. The AI picks `model_selected` skills from the conversation, so a customer can steer which skill loads. Keep sensitive content behind a skill that only a Flow loads (`flow_only`) or behind identity verification.\n\nOverrides are stored in the agent's `settings.skill_sources` (keyed by skill id) and can also be written there with `PATCH /agents/{agent_id}`, validated the same way. A `PATCH` whose `settings` leave out `skill_sources` keeps the stored overrides; `\"settings\": null` clears everything. Overrides of skills that are no longer linked are dropped, and deleting a live source removes its id from every override. A deleted Knowledge Base folder disappears from every override on read; when a skill loses all its folders, its Knowledge Base override is removed (`kb_folder_ids: []`, `kb_mode: null`), so `only` never searches nothing. Responses show each link's override in `skills[].sources` (`null` = none) with all five keys.\n\nMax 50 links. Returns `422` for a duplicate `skill_id`, an unknown `skill_id`, an archived skill, or a `pinned_version_id` that does not belong to the skill. The response is the full agent; each link shows `effective_mode`, `current_version` and `pinned_version`.\n\n## Knowledge sources\n\n| `source_type` | Extra fields | Searches |\n|---|---|---|\n| `kb_folder` | `kb_folder_id` (required) | One Knowledge Base folder |\n| `kb_all` | — | Every folder that is enabled for AI search |\n| `faq` | — | All published FAQ entries |\n| `web_search` | `allowed_domains` (optional, max 50) | The web, limited to these domains when set |\n| `live_source` | `config` (required, see below) | One web page, read live when the agent answers |\n| `lookup_table` | `config` (required, see below) | One [lookup table](#lookup-tables-api): exact matches on its search columns |\n\n```bash\ncurl -X POST \"https://api.sendseven.com/api/v1/automation/agents/AGENT_ID/knowledge-sources\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"source_type\": \"web_search\", \"allowed_domains\": [\"https://www.example.com/help\", \"docs.example.com\"]}'\n```\n\n| Field | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `source_type` | Create only, cannot be changed. |\n| `kb_folder_id` | Only for `kb_folder`. |\n| `allowed_domains` | Only for `web_search`. Entries are normalised to bare host names: `https://Www.Example.com/path` becomes `www.example.com`, `*.example.com` becomes `example.com`. IP addresses are rejected. |\n| `is_enabled` | Default `true`. A disabled source is ignored. |\n| `config` | Max 4 KB. Free-form, except for `live_source` and `lookup_table` (see below). |\n| `sort_order` | `0..10000` |\n\n### Live sources\nA `live_source` is one public page whose current content the agent reads while answering — for example opening hours, prices or stock levels that change too often for the Knowledge Base.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"source_type\": \"live_source\",\n  \"config\": {\n    \"url\": \"https://www.example.com/opening-hours\",\n    \"label\": \"Opening hours\",\n    \"description\": \"Current opening hours of all stores\",\n    \"ttl_minutes\": 15\n  }\n}\n```\n\n| `config` key | Default | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `url` | — | **Required.** Must start with `http://` or `https://` and point to a public address (private networks are rejected). Max 2048 characters. |\n| `label` | — | **Required.** 1-80 characters, unique per agent (case-insensitive). |\n| `description` | `\"\"` | Max 500 characters. Tells the agent when to read this source. |\n| `match` | `exact` | `exact` or `prefix`. |\n| `ttl_minutes` | `15` | 5-1440. How long a fetched page is reused. |\n| `fetcher` | `auto` | `auto`, `own` or `linkup_render`. |\n| `scope` | `agent` | `agent` (available on every reply, see `tools_config.live_mode`) or `skills` (only while a skill whose `sources.live_source_ids` lists it is loaded). |\n\n`allowed_domains` and `kb_folder_id` must be absent or `null`. At most 10 live sources per agent. On `PATCH`, `config` is **merged** into the stored config and validated again.\n\n### Lookup-table sources\nA `lookup_table` source gives the agent one of your [lookup tables](#lookup-tables-api). The agent then finds the matching rows in one step (for example the branch responsible for a postcode or a town) instead of searching the Knowledge Base several times.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"source_type\": \"lookup_table\",\n  \"config\": {\n    \"table_id\": \"LOOKUP_TABLE_ID\",\n    \"label\": \"Branch by postcode\",\n    \"description\": \"Use when the customer gives a postcode or town and asks which branch is responsible\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\n| `config` key | Default | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `table_id` | — | **Required.** A lookup table of this workspace. |\n| `label` | the table name | Max 80 characters. Only stored when you send it, so a renamed table shows its new name. |\n| `description` | — | Max 500 characters. Tells the agent when to use the table. |\n\n`allowed_domains` and `kb_folder_id` must be absent or `null`. At most 5 lookup tables per agent, each table once. On `PATCH`, `config` is **merged** into the stored config (send `null` for `label` or `description` to clear it) and checked again. Responses add a read-only `table` object for these sources: `{id, name, description, row_count, key_columns, version}`, or `null` when the table no longer exists. The table's **content** is not part of the agent's draft: replacing a table's file is live for every agent at once.\n\n- When the agent has **no** `kb_folder`, `kb_all` or `faq` source, it searches your whole AI-searchable Knowledge Base.\n- Conversation-history and ticket-summary folders are never searched.\n- `search_knowledge` searches up to 8 sources per call, FAQ first.\n\n`GET …/knowledge-sources` returns `{\"items\": [...], \"pagination\": {...}}` with all sources (max 20, no paging parameters).\n\n### Knowledge source errors\n\n| Status | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| `422` | `\"kb_folder_id does not exist\"` |\n| `422` | `\"This folder cannot be used as an agent knowledge source\"` (conversation-history and ticket-summary folders) |\n| `422` | `{\"code\": \"use_faq_source\", \"message\": \"…\"}` — the managed FAQ folder cannot be added as a folder. Add a `faq` source instead. |\n| `422` | `\"At most 20 knowledge sources per agent\"` |\n| `422` | `kb_folder_id` or `allowed_domains` on the wrong `source_type` |\n| `422` | `{\"code\": \"live_source_limit\", \"message\": \"…\"}` — at most 10 live sources per agent |\n| `422` | `{\"code\": \"live_source_label_taken\", \"message\": \"…\"}` — another live source of the agent has this label |\n| `422` | `{\"code\": \"invalid_live_source_url\", \"error_code\": \"…\", \"message\": \"…\"}` — the URL cannot be used (for example a private address or a host that does not exist) |\n| `422` | `{\"code\": \"invalid_live_source\", \"message\": \"…\"}` — invalid `config` on `PATCH` |\n| `422` | `{\"code\": \"lookup_table_not_found\", \"message\": \"…\"}` — `config.table_id` is not a lookup table of this workspace |\n| `422` | `{\"code\": \"lookup_table_already_attached\", \"message\": \"…\"}` — the agent already uses this table |\n| `422` | `{\"code\": \"lookup_table_source_limit\", \"message\": \"…\"}` — at most 5 lookup tables per agent |\n| `422` | `{\"code\": \"invalid_lookup_table_source\", \"message\": \"…\"}` — invalid `config` on `PATCH` |\n| `404` | `\"Knowledge source not found\"` |\n\n## Lookup tables\nA lookup table is a table you upload once (CSV or XLSX, or a table document from your Knowledge Base) and that agents search by exact value: postcode to branch, product number to price, town to district. Lookup tables belong to the workspace; attach one to an agent with a [`lookup_table` source](#lookup-table-sources). They need Conversational Agents and the Knowledge Base on your plan (Professional, Scale, Enterprise); otherwise every endpoint returns `403 {\"code\": \"feature_disabled\"}`.\n\n| Method | Path | Scope | Success |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `GET` | `/lookup-tables` | `automation:read` | `200` `{items, pagination}` |\n| `POST` | `/lookup-tables/inspect` | `automation:update` | `200` sheets of an upload, nothing stored (multipart) |\n| `POST` | `/lookup-tables` | `automation:update` | `201` table with preview (multipart upload) |\n| `POST` | `/lookup-tables/from-document` | `automation:update` **and** `knowledge_base:read` | `201` table with preview |\n| `GET` | `/lookup-tables/{table_id}` | `automation:read` | `200` table with preview |\n| `GET` | `/lookup-tables/{table_id}/rows?page=1&page_size=20` | `automation:read` | `200` `{items, pagination}` |\n| `POST` | `/lookup-tables/{table_id}/lookup` | `automation:read` | `200` lookup result |\n| `PATCH` | `/lookup-tables/{table_id}` | `automation:update` | `200` table with preview |\n| `PUT` | `/lookup-tables/{table_id}/file` | `automation:update` | `200` table with preview (new version) |\n| `POST` | `/lookup-tables/{table_id}/refresh` | `automation:update` **and** `knowledge_base:read` | `200` table with preview (new version) |\n| `DELETE` | `/lookup-tables/{table_id}` | `automation:delete` | `200` `{success, id, message}` |\n\nA table created from a Knowledge Base document holds Knowledge Base content: its `rows` and `lookup` also need `knowledge_base:read` (`403` without it), and its detail returns `preview_rows: []` without that scope.\n\n### Upload a table\n\n```bash\ncurl -X POST \"https://api.sendseven.com/api/v1/automation/lookup-tables\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\" \\\n  -F \"file=@branches.csv\" \\\n  -F \"name=Branch by postcode\" \\\n  -F 'key_columns=[\"Postcode\", \"Town\"]'\n```\n\n| Form field | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `file` | **Required.** `.csv` or `.xlsx`, at most 5 MB. CSV: UTF-8 or Windows-1252, separated by `,` `;` tab or `\\|`. XLSX: the first sheet unless `sheet` is set; formulas are not calculated (the last saved value is used). The first non-empty row is the header. |\n| `name` | **Required.** 1-120 characters, unique in the workspace (case-insensitive). |\n| `description` | Max 500 characters. |\n| `sheet` | XLSX sheet name. |\n| `key_columns` | JSON list of 1-5 column names to search by. Leave it out and SendSeven picks suggested columns. |\n| `return_columns` | JSON list of the columns the agent gets back. Empty or left out = all columns. |\n\nLimits: 20 lookup tables per workspace, 20,000 rows and 50 columns per table, 1,000 characters per cell.\n\n### Inspect a file (several sheets)\nTo import several sheets of one workbook, inspect the file first, then create one table per sheet with `POST /lookup-tables` and `sheet=<name>`. `POST /lookup-tables/inspect` (multipart, only `file`; same 5 MB limit and file checks as the upload) stores nothing:\n\n```bash\ncurl -X POST \"https://api.sendseven.com/api/v1/automation/lookup-tables/inspect\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\" \\\n  -F \"file=@pricing.xlsx\"\n```\n\n```json\n{\n  \"filename\": \"pricing.xlsx\",\n  \"file_format\": \"xlsx\",\n  \"sheets\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Branches\", \"index\": 0, \"row_count\": 912, \"column_count\": 4,\n      \"columns\": [\"Postcode\", \"Town\", \"District\", \"Branch\"],\n      \"preview_rows\": [[\"86830\", \"Schwabmünchen\", \"Augsburg\", \"Augsburg\"]],\n      \"warnings\": [], \"error\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Raw export\", \"index\": 2, \"row_count\": 0, \"column_count\": 0, \"columns\": [],\n      \"preview_rows\": [], \"warnings\": [],\n      \"error\": {\"code\": \"too_many_columns\", \"message\": \"…\", \"detail\": {\"max_columns\": 50, \"columns\": 51}}\n    }\n  ],\n  \"skipped_empty_sheets\": [\"Notes\"],\n  \"tables_remaining\": 17\n}\n```\n\n- `sheets`: one entry per sheet with content, in workbook order (`index` is the 0-based sheet position). A CSV file has exactly one entry, named after the file. `preview_rows` holds up to 5 rows in `columns` order.\n- `error`: `null` when the sheet can become a table. Otherwise the `code` that `POST /lookup-tables` with this `sheet` would return (for example `too_many_rows`, `too_many_columns`, `no_header`, `empty_table`); counts and preview then show what was read before the problem. One bad sheet never fails the whole request.\n- At most 20 sheets with content are read. Later sheets are listed with `error.code` `too_many_sheets` and no counts.\n- `skipped_empty_sheets`: sheets without a single filled cell.\n- `tables_remaining`: how many more tables the workspace can create (20 per workspace). Each sheet you import counts as one table.\n- Problems with the whole file (format, size, unreadable workbook) return the usual `422` errors below; `503 parser_busy` when too many files are being processed.\n\n`POST /lookup-tables/from-document` takes JSON `{\"kb_document_id\", \"name\", \"description\"?, \"key_columns\"?, \"return_columns\"?}`. The Knowledge Base document must be an uploaded CSV or XLSX file, or contain exactly one Markdown table. `POST /lookup-tables/{table_id}/refresh` reads that document again (only for tables created this way).\n\n### Table object\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"LOOKUP_TABLE_ID\",\n  \"name\": \"Branch by postcode\",\n  \"description\": null,\n  \"origin\": \"upload\",\n  \"kb_document_id\": null,\n  \"original_filename\": \"branches.csv\",\n  \"file_format\": \"csv\",\n  \"sheet_name\": null,\n  \"columns\": [\"Postcode\", \"Town\", \"District\", \"Branch\"],\n  \"key_columns\": [\"Postcode\", \"Town\"],\n  \"return_columns\": [],\n  \"settings\": {\"prefix\": true, \"fuzzy\": true},\n  \"row_count\": 912,\n  \"size_bytes\": 41230,\n  \"version\": 3,\n  \"agents\": [{\"id\": \"AGENT_ID\", \"name\": \"Advice Agent\"}],\n  \"created_at\": \"2026-10-22T09:00:00Z\",\n  \"updated_at\": \"2026-10-22T09:30:00Z\",\n  \"preview_rows\": [{\"row_index\": 0, \"values\": {\"Postcode\": \"86830\", \"Town\": \"Schwabmünchen\", \"District\": \"Augsburg\", \"Branch\": \"Augsburg\"}}],\n  \"warnings\": [],\n  \"suggested_key_columns\": [\"Postcode\", \"Town\"]\n}\n```\n\n- `origin`: `upload` or `kb_document`. `file_format`: `csv`, `xlsx` or `md`.\n- `return_columns`: empty = all columns. `version` goes up with every file replace or refresh.\n- `agents`: agents whose live configuration or saved draft uses the table.\n- `preview_rows` (first 20 rows), `warnings` and `suggested_key_columns` are only in single-table responses. `warnings` and `suggested_key_columns` are filled right after an upload, replace or refresh (for example a warning when a numeric column may have lost leading zeros), and empty on `GET`.\n- The list endpoint returns the same object without the three preview fields.\n\n### Change settings\n\n`PATCH /lookup-tables/{table_id}` with any of `name`, `description` (`null` clears it), `key_columns` (1-5 columns, not `null`), `return_columns` (`null` or `[]` = all columns) and `settings`:\n\n| `settings` key | Default | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `prefix` | `true` | A search also matches values that start with the query (at least 3 characters). |\n| `fuzzy` | `true` | When nothing else matches, close spellings (at least 5 characters) are offered, and the agent asks the customer to confirm. |\n\n### Replace the file\n\n`PUT /lookup-tables/{table_id}/file` (multipart: `file`, optional `sheet`, `key_columns`, `return_columns`) loads a new version. The switch is atomic and applies to every agent right away; a conversation never sees a mix of old and new rows. The current search and return columns are kept when they all still exist in the new file; otherwise send new ones or you get `422 lookup_table_columns_missing` with `missing`.\n\n### Try a lookup\n\n`POST /lookup-tables/{table_id}/lookup` with `{\"query\": \"Buxheim\", \"column\": \"Town\"}` (`column` optional) runs the same search the agent runs and costs nothing:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"match\": \"exact\",\n  \"total_matches\": 2,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"rows\": [\n    {\"row_index\": 14, \"values\": {\"Postcode\": \"87740\", \"Town\": \"Buxheim\", \"Branch\": \"Memmingen\"}, \"matched_column\": \"Town\"},\n    {\"row_index\": 15, \"values\": {\"Postcode\": \"85114\", \"Town\": \"Buxheim\", \"Branch\": \"Ingolstadt\"}, \"matched_column\": \"Town\"}\n  ],\n  \"latency_ms\": 4.2,\n  \"version\": 3\n}\n```\n\n`status`: `ok`, `no_match`, `invalid_query` or `not_found`. `match` shows which step found the rows: `exact` (the whole value), `term` (every word or number of the query, best matches first), `prefix` or `fuzzy` (confirm with the customer). Every matching row is returned, up to 20 (`truncated` is `true` when there are more), so an ambiguous value such as a town that exists twice shows all candidates.\n\n### Delete a table\n\n`DELETE /lookup-tables/{table_id}` deletes the table and all its rows. While an agent uses it (live or in a saved draft) the response is `409` with the agents to change first:\n\n```json\n{\"detail\": {\"code\": \"lookup_table_in_use\", \"message\": \"…\", \"agents\": [{\"id\": \"AGENT_ID\", \"name\": \"Advice Agent\"}]}}\n```\n\n### Lookup table errors\n\nErrors use `{\"detail\": {\"code\", \"message\", …}}`. File contents are never echoed back.\n\n| Status | `code` | When |\n|---|---|---|\n| `404` | `lookup_table_not_found` | No such table in this workspace |\n| `404` | `document_not_found` | The Knowledge Base document does not exist |\n| `409` | `lookup_table_in_use` | Delete while agents use the table (`agents`) |\n| `422` | `unsupported_format` | Not a `.csv` or `.xlsx` file |\n| `422` | `file_too_large` | Over 5 MB (`max_bytes`) |\n| `422` | `too_many_rows` / `too_many_columns` | Over 20,000 rows or 50 columns |\n| `422` | `no_header` / `empty_table` | No header row, or no data rows |\n| `422` | `invalid_encoding` / `invalid_csv` / `invalid_xlsx` | The file cannot be read |\n| `422` | `sheet_not_found` | `sheet` is not in the workbook (`sheet`, `sheets`) |\n| `422` | `too_many_sheets` | Only in an inspect sheet `error`: the workbook has more than 20 sheets with content |\n| `422` | `document_not_table` / `markdown_no_table` | The document is not a table |\n| `422` | `lookup_table_name_taken` | Another table has this name |\n| `422` | `lookup_table_limit` | 20 tables per workspace reached |\n| `422` | `invalid_key_columns` / `invalid_return_columns` | Unknown columns, more than 5 search columns, or not a JSON list |\n| `422` | `lookup_table_columns_missing` | The new file lacks configured columns (`missing`) |\n| `422` | `lookup_table_not_kb_document` | `refresh` on an uploaded table |\n| `422` | `invalid_field` / `invalid_config` | Invalid `name`, `description` or settings (`field`) |\n| `429` | `rate_limit_exceeded` | Too many files sent from this workspace (see below). Wait `retry_after` seconds (also in the `Retry-After` header) |\n| `503` | `parser_busy` | Too many files are being processed right now (inspect, create, replace, from-document, refresh). Retry in a moment |\n\nFile uploads are limited per workspace: `POST /lookup-tables/inspect` 10 per minute and 60 per hour; `POST /lookup-tables` and `PUT /lookup-tables/{table_id}/file` together 30 per minute and 200 per hour. Over a limit the response is `429` with `{\"detail\": {\"code\": \"rate_limit_exceeded\", \"error\": \"rate_limit_exceeded\", \"message\", \"retry_after\"}}` and a `Retry-After` header. When you import several sheets of one workbook, create the tables one after another; the 20-tables-per-workspace cap stays well inside the create limit.\n\n## Analytics\n\n`GET /agents/{agent_id}/analytics?days=30` (`automation:read`). `days` is `7`, `30` (default) or `90` calendar days in your workspace timezone, today included. Test-console replies are not counted. At most 20,000 replies are counted (`truncated: true` beyond).\n\n```json\n{\n  \"agent_id\": \"AGENT_ID\", \"days\": 30, \"from\": \"2026-09-06T22:00:00Z\", \"to\": \"2026-10-06T10:00:00Z\",\n  \"timezone\": \"Europe/Berlin\", \"truncated\": false,\n  \"totals\": {\"turns\": 120, \"conversations\": 64, \"answered\": 101, \"handoffs\": 12, \"fallbacks\": 4, \"errors\": 3,\n             \"kb_searches\": 140, \"kb_zero_hit_turns\": 18, \"kb_search_turns\": 110, \"web_searches\": 25, \"web_turns\": 21,\n             \"web_cache_hits\": 6, \"live_reads\": 30, \"live_turns\": 28, \"enforced_lookups\": 9,\n             \"grounding_checked\": 80, \"grounding_rewrites\": 5, \"grounding_handoffs\": 1, \"credits_billed\": 260},\n  \"rates\": {\"web_fallback_rate\": 0.175, \"kb_zero_hit_rate\": 0.1636, \"handoff_rate\": 0.1, \"grounding_rewrite_rate\": 0.075},\n  \"credits_per_conversation\": 4.06,\n  \"daily\": [{\"date\": \"2026-09-07\", \"turns\": 3, \"handoffs\": 0, \"web_searches\": 1, \"live_reads\": 1,\n             \"kb_zero_hit_turns\": 0, \"grounding_rewrites\": 0, \"credits_billed\": 7}]\n}\n```\n\n- `answered` counts answered replies and completed Flow tasks.\n- `web_fallback_rate` = `web_turns / turns`; `kb_zero_hit_rate` = `kb_zero_hit_turns / kb_search_turns`; `handoff_rate` = `handoffs / turns`; `grounding_rewrite_rate` = `(grounding_rewrites + grounding_handoffs) / grounding_checked`. A rate is `null` when its denominator is 0; `credits_per_conversation` is `null` without conversations.\n- `grounding_*` count the answer check (replies whose facts were checked against the sources). Replies sent before the check existed are not in `grounding_checked`.\n- `daily` has one entry per day, days without replies included.\n\n## Knowledge-gap suggestions\n\nQuestions the agent answered from a web search because your Knowledge Base had nothing are turned into **draft** FAQ entries for you to review. Drafts are never published automatically; review them in the FAQ list.\n\n`POST /agents/{agent_id}/kb-gap-suggestions` (`automation:update` **and** `knowledge_base:create`, because it writes FAQ drafts; missing either one returns `403`), body `{\"days\": 30}` (optional, 1-90, default 30):\n\n```json\n{\"created\": 3, \"skipped_existing\": 2, \"skipped_volatile\": 1, \"candidates\": 9, \"draft_ids\": [\"FAQ_ID_1\", \"FAQ_ID_2\", \"FAQ_ID_3\"]}\n```\n\n`skipped_existing` = already covered by your FAQ; `skipped_volatile` = answered from live sources (changing data, never drafted). One run per agent at a time: a second run returns `409 {\"code\": \"gap_run_in_progress\"}`. Starting runs is rate-limited per workspace (`429`, 3 per minute and 20 per hour). `503 {\"code\": \"kb_gap_unavailable\"}` when the feature is temporarily unavailable.\n\n`GET /agents/{agent_id}/kb-gap-suggestions?page=1&page_size=20` (`automation:read`, `page_size` max 100) lists this agent's drafts that are not archived, newest first:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"items\": [{\"id\": \"FAQ_ID_1\", \"question\": \"…\", \"answer\": \"…\", \"status\": \"draft\", \"created_at\": \"2026-10-06T08:00:00\",\n             \"review_notes\": \"…\", \"source_conversation_count\": 3}],\n  \"pagination\": {\"total\": 1, \"page\": 1, \"page_size\": 20}\n}\n```\n\n## Common errors\n\n| Status | When |\n|---|---|\n| `403` | `{\"code\": \"feature_disabled\", \"feature\": \"ai_agents\"}` (or `ai_features`; lookup tables also `knowledge_base`) — the plan does not include Conversational Agents. A missing scope returns `403 \"Insufficient scopes\"`. |\n| `404` | `\"Agent not found\"` |\n| `409` | `agent_in_use` on delete |\n| `422` | Validation errors: unknown fields, lengths, enums, JSON size limits, handoff tags, skill links, knowledge sources |\n\nSee [Billing, errors & limits](./billing-and-limits.md) for the full list.\n\n---\n\n# Drafts & Publishing\n\n:::caution Beta\nConversational Agents are in Beta and available on Professional, Scale and Enterprise.\n\nEvery agent has a **live configuration** (what contacts talk to) and at most one **draft**. Changes in the agent editor go to the draft. Contacts keep talking to the live configuration until you **publish**.\n\nBase URL: `https://api.sendseven.com/api/v1/automation`\n\n| Method | Path | Scope | Success |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `GET` | `/agents/{agent_id}/draft` | `automation:read` | `200` draft |\n| `GET` | `/agents/{agent_id}/draft/revision` | `automation:read` | `200` revision only, see [Concurrent edits](#concurrent-edits) |\n| `PUT` | `/agents/{agent_id}/draft` | `automation:update` | `200` draft |\n| `DELETE` | `/agents/{agent_id}/draft` | `automation:update` | `200` draft (now equal to live) |\n| `POST` | `/agents/{agent_id}/publish` | `automation:publish` (see [Who can publish](#who-can-publish)) | `200` agent |\n| `POST` | `/agents/{agent_id}/avatar` | `automation:update` | `200` draft |\n| `GET` | `/agents/insights/overview?days=30` | `automation:read` | `200` — see [Insights overview](#insights-overview) |\n\n:::note The regular API stays live\n`PATCH /agents/{agent_id}`, `PUT /agents/{agent_id}/skills` and the knowledge source endpoints still change the **live** agent immediately, exactly as before. Use them for automation; use the draft endpoints when you want a review step. A pending draft never overwrites those live changes when it is published (see [Merge rules](#merge-rules)).\n\n## Publish state\n\nEvery agent response carries:\n\n| Field | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `publish_state` | `published` or `draft`. `draft` means the agent has **never** been published, so it does not answer contacts yet. Agents created before drafts existed are `published`. |\n| `has_unpublished_changes` | `true` while a draft with changes exists. |\n| `draft_updated_at` | When the draft was last saved, or `null`. |\n| `published_at` | When the agent was last published, or `null`. |\n\n`POST /agents` publishes immediately by default. Send `\"publish\": false` to create a never-published agent that you finish and publish later:\n\n```json\n{\"name\": \"Shop Support\", \"instructions\": \"Answer questions about orders.\", \"publish\": false}\n```\n\n`is_active` and the bot priority are not part of the draft: they always apply instantly.\n\n## The draft document\n\n`GET /agents/{agent_id}/draft` returns:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"agent_id\": \"…\",\n  \"bot_id\": \"…\",\n  \"document\": {\"name\": \"Shop Support\", \"instructions\": \"…\", \"skills\": [], \"knowledge_sources\": [], \"activation_rules\": [], \"…\": \"…\"},\n  \"live_document\": {\"…\": \"…\"},\n  \"publish_state\": \"published\",\n  \"has_unpublished_changes\": true,\n  \"draft_updated_at\": \"2026-10-06T09:00:00Z\",\n  \"draft_updated_by_name\": \"Alex Example\",\n  \"published_at\": \"2026-10-01T12:00:00Z\",\n  \"published_by_name\": \"Alex Example\",\n  \"revision\": 12,\n  \"revision_updated_at\": \"2026-10-06T09:00:00Z\",\n  \"revision_updated_by\": {\"user_id\": \"…\", \"name\": \"Alex Example\"},\n  \"changes\": [{\"section\": \"instructions\", \"fields\": [\"instructions\"]}],\n  \"validation\": {\"errors\": [], \"warnings\": []},\n  \"avatar_display_url\": \"https://…\",\n  \"widget_connections\": [{\"widget_id\": \"…\", \"name\": \"Website chat\", \"widget_type\": \"live_chat\"}]\n}\n```\n\n| Field | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `document` | The full editable configuration: the draft when one exists, otherwise the live configuration. It accepts the same fields as [Create an agent](./agents-api.md#fields) plus `name`, `avatar_url`, the greeting button fields, `max_credits_per_conversation`, `skills`, `knowledge_sources` and `activation_rules` (channels and schedule). |\n| `live_document` | The live configuration, only while there are changes (for a side-by-side view). Otherwise `null`. |\n| `changes` | Changed fields grouped by editor section: `identity`, `instructions`, `skills`, `knowledge`, `capabilities`, `handoff`, `channels`, `settings`. |\n| `validation.errors` | What would block publishing right now (same codes as [Publish blockers](#publish-blockers)). |\n| `avatar_display_url` | A short-lived URL to show the draft's avatar. Do not store it. |\n| `widget_connections` | Active widgets that use this agent directly. Informational. |\n| `revision` | A counter that moves whenever the editable configuration changes: draft saves, publish, discard, avatar uploads and live changes through the API. See [Concurrent edits](#concurrent-edits). |\n| `revision_updated_at` / `revision_updated_by` | When and by whom the revision last moved. `revision_updated_by` is `null` for API-key and system changes. |\n\nWithout a draft, `GET` returns the live configuration with `has_unpublished_changes: false`. It never creates a draft.\n\n## Save the draft\n\n`PUT /agents/{agent_id}/draft` with `{\"document\": {…}}`.\n\n- **Send only what you change.** Fields you leave out keep their draft (or live) value. An explicit `null` resets a field to its default: `max_tool_calls`, `turn_timeout_ms`, `model_tier` and `source_footer_mode` come back as their default value, optional texts are cleared. A `null` toggle, settings section or list keeps its current value. Lists (`skills`, `knowledge_sources`, `activation_rules`) are replaced as a whole when sent. A skill link without `sources` keeps its existing per-skill source override.\n- **New list items get a server id.** Omit `id` (or send any unknown value) on a new knowledge source or activation rule. The response contains the id the server assigned; publishing creates the item with that id.\n- **No change, no draft.** If the saved draft ends up identical to the live configuration, the draft is removed and `has_unpublished_changes` is `false`. Sending back the `document` from `GET` unchanged is always a no-op.\n- Unknown fields are rejected (`422`). The same checks as the live endpoints apply (skills, tags, KB folders, live source URLs, lookup tables and limits). Where SendSeven can tell which field failed, the `422` detail also has `field` (the document path, for example `knowledge_sources.<id>.url`) and `section` (the editor section); such errors without a code of their own use `\"code\": \"invalid_field\"`. A blank `name` is allowed in a draft but blocks publishing.\n- `avatar_url` cannot be set to a new value here (`422 invalid_avatar_url`); use the [avatar upload](#avatar). Sending the current value or `null` (remove) is fine.\n- A draft is limited to 1 MB (`422 draft_too_large`).\n\n`DELETE /agents/{agent_id}/draft` throws the draft away and returns the live configuration.\n\n## Concurrent edits\nTwo people (or two browser tabs) can edit the same agent. To avoid silently overwriting each other, send the `revision` you last saw as `base_revision`:\n\n| Call | Where `base_revision` goes |\n|---|---|\n| `PUT /agents/{agent_id}/draft` | Body: `{\"document\": {…}, \"base_revision\": 12}` |\n| `POST /agents/{agent_id}/publish` | Optional body: `{\"base_revision\": 12}` |\n| `DELETE /agents/{agent_id}/draft` | Query: `?base_revision=12` |\n| `POST /agents/{agent_id}/avatar` | Form field `base_revision` |\n\nIf the agent changed since that revision, the call changes nothing and returns `409`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"detail\": {\n    \"code\": \"draft_conflict\",\n    \"message\": \"This agent was changed by someone else\",\n    \"revision\": 13,\n    \"revision_updated_at\": \"2026-10-06T09:05:00Z\",\n    \"revision_updated_by\": {\"user_id\": \"…\", \"name\": \"Alex Example\"},\n    \"current\": {\"agent_id\": \"…\", \"document\": {\"…\": \"…\"}, \"revision\": 13, \"…\": \"…\"}\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`current` is the full draft response as it is now. Show it, or save your document again with `base_revision` set to the new `revision` to overwrite.\n\n- A save that would not change anything never conflicts and does not move the revision.\n- Without `base_revision` the last write wins, as before.\n- `GET /agents/{agent_id}/draft/revision` returns only `agent_id`, `revision`, `revision_updated_at` and `revision_updated_by`. It is cheap enough to poll while an editor is open.\n\n## Test the draft\n\n`POST /agents/{agent_id}/test` accepts `\"config\": \"draft\"` to run the turn on the draft instead of the live configuration (default `\"live\"`). The response echoes which one ran in `config`. Without a draft the live configuration is used and `config` is `live`. See [Test console](./test-console-and-turns.md).\n\n## Publish\n\n`POST /agents/{agent_id}/publish` makes the draft live in one step and returns the updated agent. To see everything that still stands between an agent and going live (blockers, knowledge indexing, channels, flows) in one call, use the [readiness check](./readiness.md). If there is no draft, it only marks a never-published agent as published.\n\n### Who can publish\nPutting an agent live (it starts answering customers) needs the `automation:publish` scope. That covers:\n\n- `POST /agents/{agent_id}/publish`\n- `POST /agents` with `\"publish\": true` (the default)\n- switching a paused agent on: `PATCH /agents/{agent_id}` or `PATCH /bots/{bot_id}` with `\"is_active\": true`\n- attaching the agent to channels or widgets: `POST /bots/{bot_id}/rules` and `PUT /bots/{bot_id}/rules/{rule_id}` on the agent's bot\n\nEvery caller needs `automation:publish`: a team member through their role, an OAuth app (for example an MCP client) through its granted scopes, an API token through its own scopes. `automation:update` alone lets you build and test agents, but you must create them with `\"publish\": false` and cannot publish them, switch them on or attach them to channels.\n\nRoles, API tokens and app connections that had `automation:update` when `automation:publish` was introduced were given `automation:publish` automatically. New custom roles and tokens get it only when you select it.\n\nA caller without `automation:publish` can still create agents with `\"publish\": false`: they are always created paused (`is_active: false`), whatever `is_active` says, because a paused draft cannot answer anyone, even from a Flow.\n\nPausing an agent (`\"is_active\": false`), removing a channel rule and editing a live agent need only `automation:update`. Sending `\"is_active\": true` for an agent that is already on is not a publish.\n\nWithout the scope, nothing is changed and the response is `403`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"detail\": {\n    \"code\": \"agent_publish_not_allowed\",\n    \"message\": \"This app was not granted the automation:publish permission. It can build and test agents; publishing needs that permission.\",\n    \"required_scopes\": [\"automation:publish\"]\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Merge rules\nThe draft remembers the live configuration it started from. On save and on publish, SendSeven combines three versions: that starting point, your draft and the current live configuration.\n\n- A field you changed in the draft wins.\n- A field you did not change keeps its **current** live value, even if someone changed it through the API after the draft was started.\n- Settings objects (`tools_config`, `handoff_config`, `language_settings`, …) are combined key by key.\n- Lists are combined item by item (skills by `skill_id`, sources and rules by `id`): items you added or removed in the draft are added or removed; items added live in the meantime are kept.\n\n### Publish blockers\nIf the draft cannot go live, nothing is changed and the response is:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"detail\": {\n    \"code\": \"publish_blocked\",\n    \"message\": \"The agent cannot be published yet\",\n    \"errors\": [\n      {\"section\": \"identity\", \"field\": \"ai_disclosure_message\", \"code\": \"ai_disclosure_missing\"}\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```\n\n| `code` | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `name_missing` | The agent has no name. |\n| `ai_disclosure_missing` | The AI notice is set but has no text (only placeholders or spaces). Leave it empty to use the built-in notice. |\n| `live_source_invalid` | A live source is incomplete or its URL is not allowed. |\n| `lookup_table_not_found` | A lookup-table source points to a table that was deleted. Remove the source or pick another table. |\n| `skill_missing` | A linked skill was deleted or archived. |\n| `goal_invalid` | A linked skill's goal is invalid. |\n| `handoff_schedule_empty` | Hand-off hours are `custom` with the schedule switched on but no time ranges (section `handoff`). See [Team availability](./handoff-and-assignment.md#team-availability). |\n\n## Avatar\n`POST /agents/{agent_id}/avatar` — `multipart/form-data` with one `file`. The image goes into the **draft**; it is shown to contacts after you publish.\n\n- Optional form field `base_revision`, see [Concurrent edits](#concurrent-edits).\n- PNG, JPEG or WebP only, max 2 MB. The file content must match its type; SVG is not accepted.\n- Errors: `422 {\"code\": \"invalid_avatar\", \"message\": \"…\"}`.\n\nReturns the draft (`document.avatar_url` and `avatar_display_url` set).\n\n## Insights overview\n`GET /agents/insights/overview?days=30` — workspace-wide numbers for all agents, or one agent with `&agent_id=AGENT_ID`. `days` is `7`, `30` (default) or `90`; anything else returns `422 invalid_days`. Test-console turns are not counted.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"range_days\": 30,\n  \"goals\": [\n    {\"skill_id\": \"…\", \"skill_title\": \"Book a demo\", \"goal_label\": \"Collect name, company and preferred date\", \"started\": 42, \"completed\": 30, \"completion_pct\": 71.4}\n  ],\n  \"skills_by_usage\": [\n    {\"skill_id\": \"…\", \"title\": \"Order returns\", \"turns\": 310, \"agents\": 2}\n  ],\n  \"kb_gaps\": {\"pending_count\": 5},\n  \"truncated\": false\n}\n```\n\n| Field | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `goals` | Per skill with a [goal](./skill-goals.md): goal runs started and completed in the window. `completion_pct` is `null` when nothing started. |\n| `skills_by_usage` | Replies that used the skill and how many agents used it. |\n| `kb_gaps.pending_count` | Knowledge-gap FAQ drafts waiting for review. |\n| `truncated` | `true` when the window had more than 20,000 replies; only the newest were counted. |\n\nPer-agent conversation, hand-off and credit totals are on [`/hub/stats`](./agents-api.md#agents-overview) and [`/agents/{agent_id}/analytics`](./agents-api.md#analytics).\n\n---\n\n# Agent Readiness\n\n:::caution Beta\nConversational Agents are in Beta and available on Professional, Scale and Enterprise.\n\n`GET /agents/{agent_id}/readiness` answers one question: **can this agent go live, and what should happen next?** It combines everything you would otherwise read from several endpoints, and ends with a short, ordered `next_steps` list. It is built for setup assistants and LLM clients (for example the SendSeven MCP server), but any integration can use it.\n\nBase URL: `https://api.sendseven.com/api/v1/automation`\n\n| Method | Path | Scope | Success |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `GET` | `/agents/{agent_id}/readiness` | `automation:read` | `200` readiness |\n\nThe call is read-only and never uses AI credits. Another workspace's agent returns `404`.\n\n## Response\n\n```json\n{\n  \"agent_id\": \"…\",\n  \"bot_id\": \"…\",\n  \"name\": \"Shop Support\",\n  \"ready\": true,\n  \"live\": false,\n  \"plan\": {\n    \"allowed\": true, \"ai_agents\": true, \"ai_features\": true, \"ai_enabled\": true,\n    \"missing_features\": [], \"message\": null, \"upgrade_url\": null\n  },\n  \"publish\": {\n    \"publish_state\": \"draft\",\n    \"has_unpublished_changes\": false,\n    \"revision\": 3,\n    \"published_at\": null,\n    \"draft_updated_at\": null,\n    \"changes\": [],\n    \"blockers\": [],\n    \"can_publish\": true\n  },\n  \"knowledge\": {\n    \"sources\": [\n      {\"id\": \"…\", \"source_type\": \"kb_folder\", \"is_enabled\": true, \"kb_folder_id\": \"…\", \"kb_folder_name\": \"Help Center\",\n       \"indexing_status\": \"processing\", \"documents\": {\"total\": 12, \"ready\": 9, \"processing\": 3, \"failed\": 0}}\n    ],\n    \"has_knowledge\": true,\n    \"documents\": {\"total\": 12, \"ready\": 9, \"processing\": 3, \"failed\": 0}\n  },\n  \"skills\": {\n    \"count\": 3,\n    \"foundation_present\": true,\n    \"foundation_skill_id\": \"…\",\n    \"items\": [\n      {\"skill_id\": \"…\", \"title\": \"Shop basics\", \"mode\": \"always_on\", \"is_foundation\": true,\n       \"missing\": false, \"has_goal\": false, \"goal_valid\": null}\n    ]\n  },\n  \"capabilities\": {\n    \"search_knowledge\": true, \"web_search_mode\": \"kb_fallback\", \"live_mode\": \"off\",\n    \"read_images\": true, \"read_documents\": false, \"read_voice_messages\": false,\n    \"max_images_per_message\": 3, \"max_attachments_per_conversation\": 10\n  },\n  \"routing\": {\n    \"is_active\": true,\n    \"status\": \"draft\",\n    \"connected\": true,\n    \"activation_rules\": [{\"id\": \"…\", \"channel_types\": [\"whatsapp\"], \"is_active\": true, \"…\": \"…\"}],\n    \"all_channels\": false,\n    \"channels\": [{\"channel_id\": \"…\", \"channel_type\": \"whatsapp\", \"name\": \"Shop WhatsApp\"}],\n    \"widgets\": [],\n    \"flows\": [{\"id\": \"…\", \"name\": \"Order help\", \"status\": \"published\", \"node_ids\": [\"n4\"]}],\n    \"flow_targeted\": true\n  },\n  \"next_steps\": [\n    {\"code\": \"wait_for_indexing\", \"message\": \"Some documents are still being indexed. …\", \"endpoint\": null},\n    {\"code\": \"test_agent\", \"message\": \"Try the configuration in the test console before publishing.\", \"endpoint\": \"POST /api/v1/automation/agents/…/test\"},\n    {\"code\": \"publish\", \"message\": \"Publish the agent so contacts get this configuration. …\", \"endpoint\": \"POST /api/v1/automation/agents/…/publish\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n| Field | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `ready` | `true` when nothing blocks the agent from going live: the plan includes agents, AI is switched on for the workspace and there are no publish blockers. Steps like publishing or connecting a channel may still be needed; see `next_steps`. |\n| `live` | `true` when the agent answers contacts right now (published, active and reachable). |\n| `plan` | `ai_enabled` is the workspace's own AI switch (Settings > AI). When it is off the agent cannot answer, `ready` is `false` and the first step is `enable_ai`. |\n| `publish` | `blockers` are exactly the checks of [`POST /agents/{agent_id}/publish`](./drafts-and-publishing.md#publish-blockers), run on the draft when one exists, otherwise on the live configuration. Each blocker has `code`, `section`, `field` and a readable `message`. |\n| `knowledge.sources[].indexing_status` | `ready`, `processing` (documents still being indexed), `partially_failed`, `failed`, `empty` (no documents yet), `folder_missing` (the folder was deleted), `unknown` (could not be checked) or `not_applicable` (web search, live sources, lookup tables). Folder sources include their subfolders. |\n| `skills` | The foundation skill is the first `always_on` skill (or the only skill). `missing` = deleted or archived. `goal_valid` is `null` for skills without a [goal](./skill-goals.md). |\n| `routing.status` | Same as the agents list: `draft` (never published), `paused`, `not_connected` or `live`. |\n| `routing.activation_rules` / `channels` | The channel rules of the configuration (the draft when one exists) and the active channels they name. `all_channels` is `true` when a rule has no channel filter. |\n| `routing.widgets` / `flows` | Active widgets that start this agent, and flows (not archived) whose [Run AI Agent node](./flows.md) targets it. `flow_targeted` is `true` when one of those flows is published. |\n\nKnowledge, skills, capabilities and channel rules describe the configuration that goes live on the next publish: the draft when one exists, otherwise the live configuration.\n\n## Next steps\n\n`next_steps` is ordered: do the first one first. Each step has a stable `code`, a `message` you can show or pass to an LLM, and an `endpoint` when one API call does the step.\n\n| Code | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `enable_ai` | AI is switched off for the workspace. |\n| `fix_<blocker code>` | A publish blocker, for example `fix_skill_missing` or `fix_name_missing`. |\n| `add_skill` / `add_foundation_skill` | The agent has no skills, or no always-on skill. |\n| `add_knowledge` | No knowledge source and no web search. |\n| `fix_missing_folder`, `add_documents`, `wait_for_indexing`, `retry_failed_documents` | Knowledge Base sources that are not usable yet. |\n| `test_agent`, `publish` | The agent was never published or has unpublished changes. Publishing with an API key or app needs the `automation:publish` scope. |\n| `activate` | The agent is paused. Switching it on with an API key or app needs the `automation:publish` scope. |\n| `connect_channel` | No channel rule, widget or published flow reaches the agent. |\n| `monitor` | Nothing left to do: the agent is live. |\n\n## Plan without Conversational Agents\n\nWhen the plan does not include Conversational Agents, the call returns `403` like every other agent endpoint, with extra help in the body:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"detail\": {\n    \"code\": \"feature_disabled\",\n    \"feature\": \"ai_agents\",\n    \"missing_features\": [\"ai_agents\", \"ai_features\"],\n    \"message\": \"Conversational Agents are not included in this workspace's plan. They are available on Professional, Scale and Enterprise. Upgrade the plan to use this agent.\",\n    \"upgrade_url\": \"/settings/billing\",\n    \"next_steps\": [{\"code\": \"upgrade_plan\", \"message\": \"…\", \"endpoint\": null}]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nIf the plan cannot be checked, the call also returns this `403` (it never assumes access)."
  },
  "run-assistant-node": {
    "title": "Run AI Assistant node (agents in flows)",
    "description": "How a flow starts a Conversational Agent: the run_assistant node, its target, skill/task/output schema and branches.",
    "text": "# Agents in Flows\n\n:::caution Beta\nConversational Agents are in Beta and available on Professional, Scale and Enterprise.\n\nThe [Run AI Assistant](../../flows/nodes.md#run-ai-assistant) node hands the conversation to an agent for a bounded conversation, then continues the Flow on a branch. With an agent target, the node can:\n\n- start the agent with a specific **skill**,\n- give it a **task** (a goal prompt, input variables and an output schema), or use the skill's own **[goal](./skill-goals.md)**,\n- write the results to **flow variables** and continue on the `done` branch.\n\n## Node configuration\n\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"run_assistant\",\n  \"config\": {\n    \"target\": {\"type\": \"agent\", \"id\": \"AGENT_ID\"},\n    \"skill_slug\": \"qualify-lead\",\n    \"goal_source\": \"skill\",\n    \"output_variable_prefix\": \"lead\",\n    \"max_turns\": 20,\n    \"timeout_enabled\": true,\n    \"timeout_seconds\": 3600,\n    \"allow_stop_keyword\": true\n  }\n}\n```\n\n| Field | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `target` | `{\"type\": \"agent\", \"id\": \"<agent id>\"}`. Use the agent `id`, not its `bot_id`. A `bot_id` (or `target.type: \"bot\"`) that belongs to an agent also runs as that agent. |\n| `skill_slug` | Optional. The skill to start with. Required for a `flow_only` skill. |\n| `goal_source` | Where the goal comes from: `skill`, `node` or `none`. See below. |\n| `goal_prompt` | Task for the agent. Max 4,000 characters. Flow variables are substituted. |\n| `input_variables` | Up to 30 values passed to the agent. |\n| `output_schema` | The results the agent must return (max 20 properties). |\n| `output_variable_prefix` | Results are written to `vars.<prefix>.<key>`. Default `agent`. `^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_]{0,63}$`; reserved variable names are rejected when saving. |\n| `max_turns`, `escalation_threshold`, `timeout_enabled`, `timeout_seconds`, `allow_stop_keyword` | As for any Run AI Assistant node. |\n\nThe full field reference, including the `output_schema` format, is on the [Run AI Assistant node](../../flows/nodes.md#run-ai-assistant) page.\n\n## Goal source\n\n| `goal_source` | The agent works towards |\n|---|---|\n| `skill` | The [goal](./skill-goals.md) of the selected skill (the agent's pinned version of it, else the current version). It replaces the node's task. Completing the goal takes the `done` branch. |\n| `node` | The node's own `goal_prompt` and `output_schema`. The agent finishes with `complete_task`. |\n| `none` | No skill goal. With a `goal_prompt`/`output_schema` the node task is used, otherwise the agent simply converses. |\n| not set | `skill` when the node sets neither `goal_prompt` nor `output_schema` properties, otherwise `node`. |\n\n- With `skill`, but the skill has no goal, the node behaves as without a skill goal.\n- The goal is fixed when the agent starts. Editing the skill later does not change a running session.\n- With a skill goal, completion actions still run (save fields, assign, webhook), but `after` is skipped: the Flow continues on `done`.\n\n## Output variables\n\nWhen the agent completes, its results are merged into the flow variables:\n\n| Variable | Content |\n|---|---|\n| `{{vars.<prefix>.<key>}}` | One per output key (task outputs or goal outputs) |\n| `{{vars.<prefix>.goal_completed}}` | `true` when a skill goal was completed |\n\nOnly scalar values are written; text is cut at 4,000 characters, at most 20 keys, and multi-select answers at 50 items.\n\n## Branches\n\n| Branch | When |\n|---|---|\n| `done` | The agent completed its task or skill goal. Falls back to `completed` when not wired. |\n| `completed` | `max_turns` reached, or the session ended. |\n| `escalated` | The agent handed over to a human, or the contact used a stop keyword. Also taken when the agent **cannot start**. Falls back to `completed`. |\n| `timeout` | The contact did not reply within `timeout_seconds`. |\n\n### When the agent cannot start\n\n| Error code | Cause |\n|---|---|\n| `plan_not_allowed` | The plan does not include Conversational Agents |\n| `agent_not_found` | The agent was deleted |\n| `bot_not_found` | The agent's bot record is missing |\n| `bot_inactive` | The agent is **inactive** |\n\nThe node then takes `escalated` (or `completed` if `escalated` is not wired). With `escalated` wired, the conversation is opened for your team as an escalation.\n\n## Publish checks\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| The plan does not include Conversational Agents | Error |\n| The agent does not exist | Error |\n| `skill_slug` does not exist or is archived | Error |\n| The agent is inactive | Warning: the node \"will take its 'escalated' (or 'completed') branch until it is activated\" |\n| `goal_source: \"skill\"` but the skill has no goal for this agent | Warning |\n| A branch is not wired | Warning |\n\n## Test before publishing\n\nSimulate the node in the [test console](./test-console-and-turns.md) by sending `skill_slug` and `task`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"message\": \"Hi, I'd like a quote\",\n  \"skill_slug\": \"qualify-lead\",\n  \"task\": {\"goal_prompt\": \"Collect company size and budget.\", \"output_schema\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"company_size\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"budget\": {\"type\": \"number\"}}, \"required\": [\"company_size\"]}}\n}\n```\n\nWhen the agent completes, the response has `outcome: \"task_completed\"` and the results in `outputs`.\n\n---\n\n## Run AI Assistant\n\nHands the conversation to an AI bot for a bounded conversational sub-loop. The target is either a classic **FAQ Bot** (a Knowledge-Base-backed bot) or a [Conversational Agent (Beta)](../bots/agents/overview.md). The bot answers the contact's messages until an exit condition fires, then the flow resumes on the matching branch.\n\n| Field | Type | Notes |\n|-------|------|-------|\n| `target` | `object` | `{\"type\": \"bot\" \\| \"agent\", \"id\": \"<uuid>\"}`. Takes precedence over `bot_id`. For `agent`, `id` is the agent's `id` and is required to publish. |\n| `bot_id` | `uuid` | Legacy way to pick an FAQ Bot. Used when `target` is not set. A `bot_id` that belongs to a Conversational Agent also runs as that agent. |\n| `max_turns` | `integer \\| null` | Answered contact turns before the `completed` branch fires. `null` = unlimited (use with care). Valid range: `1..200`. |\n| `allow_stop_keyword` | `boolean` | If `true`, the contact can type one of the bot's escalation keywords (e.g. \"human\") to leave the loop via `escalated`. |\n| `timeout_enabled` / `timeout_seconds` | `boolean` / `integer` | When enabled, the `timeout` branch fires if the contact does not reply within `timeout_seconds`. Keep it within the channel's session window (24h on WhatsApp) to avoid needing a template on resume. |\n| `escalation_threshold` | `integer` | Number of unanswerable attempts before the bot escalates, for this node only. `0` turns escalation off for this node. Unset = the bot's own setting. |\n| `treat_handoff_text_as_first_message` | `boolean` | Default `false`. When `true`, the bot sends its greeting and then answers the contact's most recent message before the node (the message that started the flow, or a button tap or reply earlier in the run) as turn 1 of `max_turns`. The message does not need text: a voice message, image or PDF is forwarded when the bot can act on it (a Conversational Agent reads images and PDFs and transcribes voice messages when voice reading is on, otherwise it replies that it cannot listen to voice messages; an FAQ Bot reads images with image reading on and asks for text on a voice message). Stickers, GIFs, videos and other files without text are not forwarded and do not count as a turn. No effect for routing, lead-qualification or appointment bots. |\n\n**Agent-only fields.** With `target.type: \"agent\"` the node can give the agent a **task** and collect structured results:\n\n| Field | Type | Notes |\n|-------|------|-------|\n| `skill_slug` | `string` | Start the agent with this [skill](../bots/agents/overview.md#skills) loaded. Same format as a skill slug. |\n| `goal_source` | `string` | `skill`, `node` or `none`. `skill` uses the selected skill's [goal](../bots/agents/skill-goals.md) instead of `goal_prompt`/`output_schema`; `node` uses the node's own task; `none` never uses a skill goal. When not set: `skill` if the node defines neither `goal_prompt` nor `output_schema` properties, otherwise `node`. See [Agents in Flows](../bots/agents/flows.md#goal-source). |\n| `goal_prompt` | `string` | What the agent should achieve. Max 4,000 characters. Flow variables are substituted. |\n| `input_variables` | `object` | Up to 30 values passed to the agent. Keys: `^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]{0,63}$`. Values: text (max 2,000 characters, variables substituted), number, boolean or `null`. |\n| `output_schema` | `object` | The results the agent must return. See below. |\n| `output_variable_prefix` | `string` | Where the results are written: `vars.<prefix>.<key>`. Default `agent`. Pattern `^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_]{0,63}$`; reserved variable names are rejected. |\n\n`output_schema` is a small JSON Schema object (max 8 KB):\n\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"description\": \"Return request details\",\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"order_number\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"description\": \"The order to return\"},\n    \"reason\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"damaged\", \"wrong_size\", \"other\"]},\n    \"items\": {\"type\": \"integer\"},\n    \"refund\": {\"type\": \"boolean\"}\n  },\n  \"required\": [\"order_number\", \"reason\"]\n}\n```\n\n- Max 20 properties. Names match `^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_]{0,63}$`.\n- Each property has a `type` of `string`, `number`, `integer` or `boolean`, an optional `description` (max 500 characters) and an optional `enum` (1-50 scalar values).\n- `required` may only list declared properties.\n\nWhen the agent has collected everything, it completes the task and the node leaves on the `done` branch. The results are available to every later node as `{{vars.<prefix>.<key>}}`, for example `{{vars.agent.order_number}}`. Only declared properties with scalar values are written (text is cut at 4,000 characters), and they are merged into the existing variables.\n\n**Branches.**\n\n| Branch | Fires when |\n|--------|------------|\n| `completed` | The bot answered `max_turns` turns, or ended the session itself. |\n| `done` | **Agent targets only.** The agent completed its task; outputs are in `vars.<prefix>.*`. If not wired, the run continues on `completed`. Wiring `done` on a non-agent target is rejected when saving. |\n| `escalated` | The bot handed the conversation to a human, or the contact used a stop keyword. Also taken when the target cannot start: the bot is missing or inactive, or the plan does not include Conversational Agents (error `plan_not_allowed`). Falls back to `completed` when not wired. |\n| `timeout` | No contact reply within `timeout_seconds`, or the session could not be set up. |\n\nMissing branches are **publish-time warnings**, not errors. Publishing also warns when two output property names differ only in case. Wire every branch for predictable production behavior.\n\n---\n\n### `run_assistant`\n\nHands the conversation to an AI bot or Conversational Agent until it finishes.\n\n| Field | Type / allowed values | Required | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `bot_id` | bot id | yes for a bot | Must be a bot of this workspace. |\n| `target` | `{type, id}` | no | `type`: `agent` / `bot`. |\n| `timeout_seconds` | integer | no |  |\n| `max_turns` | integer 1..200 | no |  |\n| `allow_stop_keyword` | boolean | no |  |\n| `output_variable_prefix` | identifier | no | Prefix for the values the assistant extracts. |\n\n**Branches:** `completed` / `default` / `done` / `escalated` / `timeout`. `done` is only valid when the target is an agent."
  }
};
